window.INSCRIPTIONS_ATLAS={"records":[{"list_order":"1","inscription_name":"Allahabad / Prayaga pillar inscription","date_display":"Fourth century CE","present_place":"Prayagraj Fort, Prayagraj district, Uttar Pradesh, India","latitude":25.431111,"longitude":81.875,"coordinate_precision":"Published pillar coordinate","date_start_ce":350.0,"date_end_ce":399.0,"material":"Stone pillar","king_or_named_person":"Samudragupta","findspot":"Prayagraj, Uttar Pradesh","event_or_recorded_subject":"Names Kamarupa and Davaka among frontier kingdoms in a Gupta imperial eulogy.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Contemporary outside attestation; claims of submission belong to the imperial praise text.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"E01","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"External reference / identification","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Introduction notice image checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.15; supplied PDF p. 32","summary_file":"External inscriptions/E01-Allahabad-Prayaga-pillar-inscription.txt","coordinate_type":"monument","coordinate_reference":"Prayaga pillar in Prayagraj Fort","location_source":"https://siddham.network/object/ob00001/","location_notes":"Coordinate refers to the present pillar location; theories about its ancient relocation are separate.","summary_text":"Allahabad / Prayaga pillar inscription\n\nDate: Fourth century CE\nRuler or persons named: Samudragupta\nPlace: Prayagraj, Uttar Pradesh\n\nSUMMARY\nNames Kamarupa and Davaka among frontier kingdoms in a Gupta imperial eulogy.\n\nHOW IT RELATES TO KAMARUPA\nContemporary outside attestation; claims of submission belong to the imperial praise text.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.15, supplied PDF p. 32. This summary follows Sharma’s notice; the full external edition has not been re-edited here.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/External inscriptions/E01-Allahabad-Prayaga-pillar-inscription.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"2","inscription_name":"Umachal Rock Inscription Surendravarman","date_display":"Fifth century CE; no explicit calendar date.","present_place":"Umachal, northeastern Nilachal / Kamakhya hill, Guwahati, Assam, India","latitude":26.166,"longitude":91.706,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — nearby reference, not exact findspot","date_start_ce":400.0,"date_end_ce":499.0,"material":"Rock / stone","king_or_named_person":"Surendravarman; his identification with a ruler of the Varman genealogy is debated.","findspot":"Umachal, on the Kamakhya hill at Guwahati.","event_or_recorded_subject":"A short Sanskrit rock inscription records that Maharajadhiraja Surendravarman caused a cave shrine for the deity Balabhadra to be made. It commemorates a religious construction rather than a land grant.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S01","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 55-57","summary_file":"01-Umachal-Rock-Inscription-Surendravarman.txt","coordinate_type":"nearby_landmark","coordinate_reference":"Kamakhya temple, Nilachal hill","location_source":"https://kamakhya.org/introduction/","location_notes":"Nearby hill landmark only. The Umachal inscription is on the northeastern slope; this is not its surveyed position.","summary_text":"Umachal Rock Inscription Surendravarman\n\nDate: Fifth century CE; no explicit calendar date.\nRuler: Surendravarman; his identification with a ruler of the Varman genealogy is debated.\nPlace: Umachal, on the Kamakhya hill at Guwahati.\n\nSUMMARY\nA short Sanskrit rock inscription records that Maharajadhiraja Surendravarman caused a cave shrine for the deity Balabhadra to be made. It commemorates a religious construction rather than a land grant.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nIt is early direct evidence of royal religious patronage, Sanskrit writing and the eastern Gupta script in the Guwahati area. The ruler’s name is secure, but equating him with another similarly named king is an interpretation.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 55-57.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/01-Umachal-Rock-Inscription-Surendravarman.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"3","inscription_name":"Nagajari Khanikargaon Fragmentary Stone Inscription","date_display":"Early fifth century CE according to Sharma; other scholars proposed later dates.","present_place":"Nagajari-Khanikargaon, Sarupathar area, Golaghat district, Assam, India","latitude":26.194,"longitude":93.866,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — nearby reference, not exact findspot","date_start_ce":400.0,"date_end_ce":499.0,"material":"Rock / stone","king_or_named_person":"No identifiable reigning king survives.","findspot":"Nagajari-Khanikargaon, Sarupathar area, Golaghat region.","event_or_recorded_subject":"A fragmentary stone inscription preserves portions of a record apparently concerning land and boundaries. Names including Brahmadatta and Vaji occur, but their roles are debated. Sharma considers them possible officials, witnesses or boundary references rather than securely identified recipients.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE. Palaeographic date or reading is disputed; see summary.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"SA01","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 357-359","summary_file":"A01-Nagajari-Khanikargaon-Fragmentary-Stone-Inscription.txt","coordinate_type":"nearby_town","coordinate_reference":"Sarupathar town","location_source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sarupathar","location_notes":"Town reference for the Nagajari-Khanikargaon / Sarupathar area, not the stone recovery point.","summary_text":"Nagajari Khanikargaon Fragmentary Stone Inscription\n\nDate: Early fifth century CE according to Sharma; other scholars proposed later dates.\nRuler: No identifiable reigning king survives.\nPlace: Nagajari-Khanikargaon, Sarupathar area, Golaghat region.\n\nSUMMARY\nA fragmentary stone inscription preserves portions of a record apparently concerning land and boundaries. Names including Brahmadatta and Vaji occur, but their roles are debated. Sharma considers them possible officials, witnesses or boundary references rather than securely identified recipients.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nPotentially among the earliest epigraphic evidence from Assam. Its fragmentary condition prevents a complete reconstruction of the transaction, donor or ruler; the date is based on script rather than an explicit surviving year.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 357-359.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/A01-Nagajari-Khanikargaon-Fragmentary-Stone-Inscription.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"4","inscription_name":"Duboroni stone inscription","date_display":"Approximately 400–500 CE, palaeographic attribution","present_place":"Duboroni / Dubarani, Borpathar–Sarupathar area, Golaghat district, Assam, India","latitude":26.277,"longitude":93.902,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — nearby reference, not exact findspot","date_start_ce":400.0,"date_end_ce":500.0,"material":"Stone slab","king_or_named_person":"Sri Vasundharavarman","findspot":"Duboroni, Borpathar, Golaghat; collected about 2 km northwest of the excavated mound","event_or_recorded_subject":"The excavation report describes a one-line stone inscription measuring 44 × 23 × 8.8 cm, beginning with a trident sign. Its script is compared with Nagajari-Khanikargaon. The reported reading names Sri Vasundharavarman.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"The report calls him perhaps a regional king and infers a Shaiva affiliation. It does not establish his place in the Guwahati Varman dynasty. A wharf interpretation occurs in later teaching material but is not established by the passage checked here. Discovery date is not equated with the report year.","source_url":"https://assets.ctfassets.net/l47hkrce4wip/1QK88bFQMLUHt301oLRfIP/66a33097d420ddc59c5d761ace1ed64d/011_Indian_Archaeology_A_Review_1997-98.pdf","record_id":"B05","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Regional / local inscription","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Official record checked","source_locator":"Archaeological Survey of India, Indian Archaeology 1997–98—A Review (published 2003), printed pp.6,8–9; PDF pp.14,16–17","summary_file":"Supplementary inscriptions/B05-Duboroni-stone-inscription.txt","coordinate_type":"nearby_town","coordinate_reference":"Barpathar town","location_source":"https://mapcarta.com/14899416","location_notes":"Nearby town reference for the Duboroni archaeological region; not the excavated mound or the slab collection point.","summary_text":"Duboroni stone inscription\n\nDate: Approximately 400–500 CE, palaeographic attribution\nRuler or named person: Sri Vasundharavarman\nPlace: Duboroni, Borpathar, Golaghat; collected about 2 km northwest of the excavated mound\n\nSUMMARY\nThe excavation report describes a one-line stone inscription measuring 44 × 23 × 8.8 cm, beginning with a trident sign. Its script is compared with Nagajari-Khanikargaon. The reported reading names Sri Vasundharavarman.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nAdds early written evidence from the Dhansiri valley. It is documented in a report covering fieldwork well after Sharma’s publication.\n\nLIMITS OF THE EVIDENCE\nThe report calls him perhaps a regional king and infers a Shaiva affiliation. It does not establish his place in the Guwahati Varman dynasty. A wharf interpretation occurs in later teaching material but is not established by the passage checked here. Discovery date is not equated with the report year.\n\nSource: Archaeological Survey of India, Indian Archaeology 1997–98—A Review (published 2003), printed pp.6,8–9; PDF pp.14,16–17\nSource location: https://assets.ctfassets.net/l47hkrce4wip/1QK88bFQMLUHt301oLRfIP/66a33097d420ddc59c5d761ace1ed64d/011_Indian_Archaeology_A_Review_1997-98.pdf\nEvidence checked: Official record checked\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/Supplementary inscriptions/B05-Duboroni-stone-inscription.txt","source_href":"https://assets.ctfassets.net/l47hkrce4wip/1QK88bFQMLUHt301oLRfIP/66a33097d420ddc59c5d761ace1ed64d/011_Indian_Archaeology_A_Review_1997-98.pdf"},{"list_order":"5","inscription_name":"Barganga Rock Inscription Bhutivarman","date_display":"Sixth century CE; precise dating is disputed.","present_place":"Barganga archaeological site, near Dokmoka, Karbi Anglong district, Assam, India","latitude":26.208,"longitude":93.05,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — nearby reference, not exact findspot","date_start_ce":500.0,"date_end_ce":599.0,"material":"Rock / stone","king_or_named_person":"Bhutivarman, named as the reigning king.","findspot":"Beside the Barganga river, in the region described in the book as Nowgong district.","event_or_recorded_subject":"This short rock record is associated with a ruined religious establishment. Sharma interprets it as a religious foundation made by an official or district governor for the king’s welfare and longevity. The inscription is difficult to read, and proposed readings of its date differ.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S02","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 58-63","summary_file":"02-Barganga-Rock-Inscription-Bhutivarman.txt","coordinate_type":"nearby_town","coordinate_reference":"Dokmoka town","location_source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dokmoka","location_notes":"Barganga is described by Assam government as about 3 km south of Dokmoka. This point marks the town, not the inscription.","summary_text":"Barganga Rock Inscription Bhutivarman\n\nDate: Sixth century CE; precise dating is disputed.\nRuler: Bhutivarman, named as the reigning king.\nPlace: Beside the Barganga river, in the region described in the book as Nowgong district.\n\nSUMMARY\nThis short rock record is associated with a ruined religious establishment. Sharma interprets it as a religious foundation made by an official or district governor for the king’s welfare and longevity. The inscription is difficult to read, and proposed readings of its date differ.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nIt supplies contemporary evidence for Bhutivarman’s rule and for religious patronage through local officials. A precise year or a detailed reconstruction of the damaged wording should not be treated as certain.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 58-63.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/02-Barganga-Rock-Inscription-Bhutivarman.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"6","inscription_name":"Mandsaur pillar inscription of Yashodharman","date_display":"Approximately 525–535 CE in Sharma’s broad dating","present_place":"Sondani / Sondhni, near Mandsaur, Madhya Pradesh, India","latitude":24.041332,"longitude":75.091968,"coordinate_precision":"Published pillar-site coordinate","date_start_ce":525.0,"date_end_ce":535.0,"material":"Stone pillar","king_or_named_person":"Yashodharman","findspot":"Mandsaur, Madhya Pradesh","event_or_recorded_subject":"Claims an expansive sphere of victory or authority reaching the Brahmaputra.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"A reference to the river is not a local Kamarupa grant or proof of sustained administration there.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"E02","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"External reference / identification","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Introduction notice image checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.15; supplied PDF p. 32","summary_file":"External inscriptions/E02-Mandsaur-pillar-inscription-of-Yashodharman.txt","coordinate_type":"monument","coordinate_reference":"Sondani pillar site","location_source":"https://siddham.network/object/ob00086/","location_notes":"Two associated pillars share a site coordinate; this is not a campaign boundary.","summary_text":"Mandsaur pillar inscription of Yashodharman\n\nDate: Approximately 525–535 CE in Sharma’s broad dating\nRuler or persons named: Yashodharman\nPlace: Mandsaur, Madhya Pradesh\n\nSUMMARY\nClaims an expansive sphere of victory or authority reaching the Brahmaputra.\n\nHOW IT RELATES TO KAMARUPA\nA reference to the river is not a local Kamarupa grant or proof of sustained administration there.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.15, supplied PDF p. 32. This summary follows Sharma’s notice; the full external edition has not been re-edited here.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/External inscriptions/E02-Mandsaur-pillar-inscription-of-Yashodharman.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"7","inscription_name":"Dubi Copper Plates Bhaskaravarman","date_display":"Early seventh century CE; Sharma places it approximately in the first quarter.","present_place":"Dubi, near Pathsala, Bajali district, Assam, India","latitude":26.5,"longitude":91.21,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate village location","date_start_ce":600.0,"date_end_ce":649.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Bhaskaravarman, Varman dynasty.","findspot":"Dubi, near Pathsala, in the area described as Kamrup district.","event_or_recorded_subject":"A copper-plate charter gives an extensive royal genealogy and praise of Bhaskaravarman’s predecessors, followed by grant-related material. Five plates survive from an original set of six, so the transaction is incompletely preserved. Its narrative includes the earlier donation tradition associated with Bhutivarman.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S03","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 64-86","summary_file":"03-Dubi-Copper-Plates-Bhaskaravarman.txt","coordinate_type":"village","coordinate_reference":"Dubi village","location_source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dubi,_Pathsala","location_notes":"Village coordinate only; the exact digging point near the temple is not established.","summary_text":"Dubi Copper Plates Bhaskaravarman\n\nDate: Early seventh century CE; Sharma places it approximately in the first quarter.\nRuler: Bhaskaravarman, Varman dynasty.\nPlace: Dubi, near Pathsala, in the area described as Kamrup district.\n\nSUMMARY\nA copper-plate charter gives an extensive royal genealogy and praise of Bhaskaravarman’s predecessors, followed by grant-related material. Five plates survive from an original set of six, so the transaction is incompletely preserved. Its narrative includes the earlier donation tradition associated with Bhutivarman.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nA major source for the Varman royal succession, queens, royal claims and the religious setting of land grants. Its account combines dynastic history with praise and mythical ancestry; the missing final plate limits reconstruction of the grant.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 64-86.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/03-Dubi-Copper-Plates-Bhaskaravarman.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"8","inscription_name":"Seal Attached to Dubi Plates Bhaskaravarman","date_display":"Seventh century CE, associated with Bhaskaravarman’s Dubi charter.","present_place":"Dubi, near Pathsala, Bajali district, Assam, India","latitude":26.5,"longitude":91.21,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate village location","date_start_ce":600.0,"date_end_ce":649.0,"material":"Metal seal attached to copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Bhaskaravarman and his Varman predecessors.","findspot":"Attached to the Dubi copper-plate grant.","event_or_recorded_subject":"The seal has an elephant emblem and an inscription tracing the royal line from Pushyavarman through successive rulers and queens to Bhaskaravarman. It also asserts ancestry connected with Naraka, Bhagadatta and Vajradatta, and mentions horse sacrifices performed by earlier kings.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S04","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 87-88","summary_file":"04-Seal-Attached-to-Dubi-Plates-Bhaskaravarman.txt","coordinate_type":"village","coordinate_reference":"Dubi village","location_source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dubi,_Pathsala","location_notes":"Same provenance as the Dubi plates; not the present repository.","summary_text":"Seal Attached to Dubi Plates Bhaskaravarman\n\nDate: Seventh century CE, associated with Bhaskaravarman’s Dubi charter.\nRuler: Bhaskaravarman and his Varman predecessors.\nPlace: Attached to the Dubi copper-plate grant.\n\nSUMMARY\nThe seal has an elephant emblem and an inscription tracing the royal line from Pushyavarman through successive rulers and queens to Bhaskaravarman. It also asserts ancestry connected with Naraka, Bhagadatta and Vajradatta, and mentions horse sacrifices performed by earlier kings.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nIt authenticates the charter and provides a compact genealogy useful for comparison with the Dubi plates and Nalanda seals. The mythical ancestry expresses royal legitimacy and is not independent proof of biological descent.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 87-88.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/04-Seal-Attached-to-Dubi-Plates-Bhaskaravarman.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"9","inscription_name":"Nalanda Clay Seals Bhaskaravarman","date_display":"Seventh century CE; excavated fragments were recovered in 1917–18 and 1927–28.","present_place":"Nalanda Mahavihara archaeological site, Nalanda district, Bihar, India","latitude":25.136667,"longitude":85.443889,"coordinate_precision":"Published UNESCO site reference","date_start_ce":600.0,"date_end_ce":649.0,"material":"Clay seal impressions","king_or_named_person":"Bhaskaravarman, Varman dynasty.","findspot":"Nalanda, Bihar, outside Assam.","event_or_recorded_subject":"Fragmentary clay seals recovered at Nalanda preserve a royal genealogy substantially corresponding to the seal of the Dubi charter. Sharma compares the fragments and reconstructs their wording with help from parallel records.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S05","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Kamarupa royal seal outside region","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 89-91","summary_file":"05-Nalanda-Clay-Seals-Bhaskaravarman.txt","coordinate_type":"archaeological_site","coordinate_reference":"Nalanda Mahavihara archaeological site","location_source":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1502/","location_notes":"Site reference only; not an individual seal excavation locus or museum location.","summary_text":"Nalanda Clay Seals Bhaskaravarman\n\nDate: Seventh century CE; excavated fragments were recovered in 1917–18 and 1927–28.\nRuler: Bhaskaravarman, Varman dynasty.\nPlace: Nalanda, Bihar, outside Assam.\n\nSUMMARY\nFragmentary clay seals recovered at Nalanda preserve a royal genealogy substantially corresponding to the seal of the Dubi charter. Sharma compares the fragments and reconstructs their wording with help from parallel records.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nThey independently attest the presence of Bhaskaravarman’s royal seal material at Nalanda and corroborate parts of his genealogy. They are evidence of connections beyond Assam; the seals alone do not establish political rule over Nalanda.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 89-91.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/05-Nalanda-Clay-Seals-Bhaskaravarman.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"10","inscription_name":"Nidhanpur Copper Plates Bhaskaravarman","date_display":"Approximately 620–643 CE in Sharma’s dating.","present_place":"Nidhanpur / Nidanpur, Beanibazar area, Sylhet district, Bangladesh","latitude":24.805,"longitude":92.155,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate village location","date_start_ce":620.0,"date_end_ce":643.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Bhaskaravarman; the earlier grant is attributed to Bhutivarman.","findspot":"Nidhanpur in Sylhet, now Bangladesh; issued from Karnasuvarna.","event_or_recorded_subject":"The charter renews an earlier land grant whose documentary record had been lost or destroyed by fire. It describes land in the Mayurasalmala agrahara, lists numerous Brahmin beneficiaries with their religious and genealogical affiliations, allocates shares and sets out boundaries and grant conditions.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S06","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 92-135","summary_file":"06-Nidhanpur-Copper-Plates-Bhaskaravarman.txt","coordinate_type":"village","coordinate_reference":"Nidhanpur village","location_source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nidhanpur","location_notes":"Modern village coordinate; not Karnasuvarna, the charter issuing place.","summary_text":"Nidhanpur Copper Plates Bhaskaravarman\n\nDate: Approximately 620–643 CE in Sharma’s dating.\nRuler: Bhaskaravarman; the earlier grant is attributed to Bhutivarman.\nPlace: Nidhanpur in Sylhet, now Bangladesh; issued from Karnasuvarna.\n\nSUMMARY\nThe charter renews an earlier land grant whose documentary record had been lost or destroyed by fire. It describes land in the Mayurasalmala agrahara, lists numerous Brahmin beneficiaries with their religious and genealogical affiliations, allocates shares and sets out boundaries and grant conditions.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nOne of the richest sources for Varman genealogy, Brahmin settlement, land administration and historical geography. Its place of issue is important for Bhaskaravarman’s political activity beyond the Brahmaputra valley. The long beneficiary list is especially valuable for social history.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 92-135.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/06-Nidhanpur-Copper-Plates-Bhaskaravarman.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"11","inscription_name":"Apshad stone inscription","date_display":"Seventh century CE; Sharma gives approximately 672","present_place":"Aphsad / Apshad, Nawada district, Bihar, India","latitude":24.88,"longitude":85.53,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — broad regional reference","date_start_ce":650.0,"date_end_ce":699.0,"material":"Stone","king_or_named_person":"Adityasena; Mahasenagupta and Susthitavarman named","findspot":"Apshad / Aphsad, Bihar","event_or_recorded_subject":"Praises Mahasenagupta’s defeat of Susthitavarman on the banks of the Brahmaputra.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Identification of Susthitavarman with the Kamarupa ruler is a historical interpretation.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"E03","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"External reference / identification","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Introduction notice image checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.15; supplied PDF p. 32","summary_file":"External inscriptions/E03-Apshad-stone-inscription.txt","coordinate_type":"regional_reference","coordinate_reference":"Nawada district reference","location_source":"https://nawada.nic.in/","location_notes":"Broad district reference for Aphsad. It is not a verified nearby village or inscription point; use only for regional mapping.","summary_text":"Apshad stone inscription\n\nDate: Seventh century CE; Sharma gives approximately 672\nRuler or persons named: Adityasena; Mahasenagupta and Susthitavarman named\nPlace: Apshad / Aphsad, Bihar\n\nSUMMARY\nPraises Mahasenagupta’s defeat of Susthitavarman on the banks of the Brahmaputra.\n\nHOW IT RELATES TO KAMARUPA\nIdentification of Susthitavarman with the Kamarupa ruler is a historical interpretation.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.15, supplied PDF p. 32. This summary follows Sharma’s notice; the full external edition has not been re-edited here.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/External inscriptions/E03-Apshad-stone-inscription.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"12","inscription_name":"Gauhati copper bell inscription","date_display":"Eighth century CE in published inventories","present_place":"Udalbakra / Narakasurgaon, Guwahati, Assam, India","latitude":26.143,"longitude":91.742,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — nearby reference, not exact findspot","date_start_ce":700.0,"date_end_ce":799.0,"material":"Copper bell","king_or_named_person":"Srikumara","findspot":"Udalbakra / Narakasurgaon, Guwahati","event_or_recorded_subject":"An inscribed copper bell preserves the name Srikumara. Its archaeological provenance and religious use make it distinct from the royal copper-plate charters.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"The full edited inscription has not been read in this audit. Do not infer a dynasty, royal succession or a detailed donation from the name alone. NMMA search catalogue reports two inscribed lines. Collect D. Sarma’s edition before expanding the summary.","source_url":"https://nmma.nic.in/nmma/antiqDetail.do?object=98&refId=229383","record_id":"B03","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Regional / local inscription","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Catalogue and bibliography checked","source_locator":"NMMA SM-ASM-AM-229383; D. Sarma, The Copper Bell Inscription of Srikumara; Sanyal 2014, p.218","summary_file":"Supplementary inscriptions/B03-Gauhati-copper-bell-inscription.txt","coordinate_type":"nearby_neighbourhood","coordinate_reference":"Lalganesh neighbourhood, Guwahati","location_source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lalganesh","location_notes":"Neighbourhood reference near Udalbakra / Narakasurgaon. Exact hoard discovery position is not established.","summary_text":"Gauhati copper bell inscription\n\nDate: Eighth century CE in published inventories\nRuler or named person: Srikumara\nPlace: Udalbakra / Narakasurgaon, Guwahati\n\nSUMMARY\nAn inscribed copper bell preserves the name Srikumara. Its archaeological provenance and religious use make it distinct from the royal copper-plate charters.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nEvidence for religious material culture and a named individual outside the main charter corpus.\n\nLIMITS OF THE EVIDENCE\nThe full edited inscription has not been read in this audit. Do not infer a dynasty, royal succession or a detailed donation from the name alone. NMMA search catalogue reports two inscribed lines. Collect D. Sarma’s edition before expanding the summary.\n\nSource: NMMA SM-ASM-AM-229383; D. Sarma, The Copper Bell Inscription of Srikumara; Sanyal 2014, p.218\nSource location: https://nmma.nic.in/nmma/antiqDetail.do?object=98&refId=229383\nEvidence checked: Catalogue and bibliography checked\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/Supplementary inscriptions/B03-Gauhati-copper-bell-inscription.txt","source_href":"https://nmma.nic.in/nmma/antiqDetail.do?object=98&refId=229383"},{"list_order":"13","inscription_name":"Shankara Narayana Stone Image Inscription","date_display":"Eighth century CE in the edition.","present_place":"Deopani, Assam, India","latitude":26.219709892356004,"longitude":93.82396043236585,"coordinate_precision":"User-supplied locality point; accuracy not independently assessed","date_start_ce":700.0,"date_end_ce":799.0,"material":"Inscribed stone image","king_or_named_person":"Sri Jivara in Sharma’s reading; exact dynastic placement is interpretive.","findspot":"Deopani; the larger of two inscribed composite Shiva–Vishnu images discussed in the book.","event_or_recorded_subject":"A three-line Sanskrit inscription on a composite image refers to the reigning monarch and to Shankara and Narayana. Sharma discusses whether the words name the image or refer to prayers recited during its consecration. The text contains non-classical linguistic forms and difficult readings.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE. Palaeographic date or reading is disputed; see summary.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"SA03","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 364-365","summary_file":"A03-Shankara-Narayana-Stone-Image-Inscription.txt","coordinate_type":"user_supplied_locality","coordinate_reference":"Deopani — user-identified locality","location_source":"User-provided coordinates in this conversation, 1 November 2026","location_notes":"Coordinates supplied by the user for the modern locality. This does not independently establish the exact ancient recovery point or the current repository. Decimal precision is retained as provided and is not a statement of positional accuracy.","summary_text":"Shankara Narayana Stone Image Inscription\n\nDate: Eighth century CE in the edition.\nRuler: Sri Jivara in Sharma’s reading; exact dynastic placement is interpretive.\nPlace: Deopani; the larger of two inscribed composite Shiva–Vishnu images discussed in the book.\n\nSUMMARY\nA three-line Sanskrit inscription on a composite image refers to the reigning monarch and to Shankara and Narayana. Sharma discusses whether the words name the image or refer to prayers recited during its consecration. The text contains non-classical linguistic forms and difficult readings.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nUseful for religious image worship, royal names and early devotional practices. Sharma’s proposed succession links and interpretation of the consecration language are scholarly reconstructions, not explicit complete historical statements.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 364-365.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/A03-Shankara-Narayana-Stone-Image-Inscription.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"14","inscription_name":"Harihara Stone Image Inscription","date_display":"Eighth century CE in the edition.","present_place":"Deopani, Assam, India","latitude":26.219709892356004,"longitude":93.82396043236585,"coordinate_precision":"User-supplied locality point; accuracy not independently assessed","date_start_ce":700.0,"date_end_ce":799.0,"material":"Inscribed stone image","king_or_named_person":"Diglekhavarman in Sharma’s reading.","findspot":"Deopani; the smaller of two inscribed composite Shiva–Vishnu images discussed in the book.","event_or_recorded_subject":"A short three-line inscription records an image of Harihara given during the reign of Maharajadhiraja Diglekhavarman to the son of Kalhiya, accompanied by the recitation of a hundred names of Hari, according to Sharma’s translation. The Sanskrit has irregular forms and some names are uncertain.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE. Palaeographic date or reading is disputed; see summary.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"SA04","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 366-366","summary_file":"A04-Harihara-Stone-Image-Inscription.txt","coordinate_type":"user_supplied_locality","coordinate_reference":"Deopani — user-identified locality","location_source":"User-provided coordinates in this conversation, 1 November 2026","location_notes":"Coordinates supplied by the user for the modern locality. This does not independently establish the exact ancient recovery point or the current repository. Decimal precision is retained as provided and is not a statement of positional accuracy.","summary_text":"Harihara Stone Image Inscription\n\nDate: Eighth century CE in the edition.\nRuler: Diglekhavarman in Sharma’s reading.\nPlace: Deopani; the smaller of two inscribed composite Shiva–Vishnu images discussed in the book.\n\nSUMMARY\nA short three-line inscription records an image of Harihara given during the reign of Maharajadhiraja Diglekhavarman to the son of Kalhiya, accompanied by the recitation of a hundred names of Hari, according to Sharma’s translation. The Sanskrit has irregular forms and some names are uncertain.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nEvidence for composite Shiva–Vishnu worship, image donation and devotional recitation. The suggestion that this ruler immediately followed Sri Jivara is an interpretation and should not be treated as established succession.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 366-366.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/A04-Harihara-Stone-Image-Inscription.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"15","inscription_name":"Palasini fragmentary copper plate of Jivaraja","date_display":"Probably early medieval; identification with eighth-century Jivara proposed","present_place":"Palasini / Palasani, historical Nagaon region, Assam, India; modern village match unresolved","latitude":26.35,"longitude":92.67,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — broad regional reference","date_start_ce":700.0,"date_end_ce":899.0,"material":"Copper plate fragment","king_or_named_person":"Maharajadhiraja Sri Jivaraja","findspot":"Palasini, Nagaon","event_or_recorded_subject":"The published archaeological discussion identifies a fragmentary land-grant plate naming Sri Jivaraja and a Brahmin from Kamarupa. It cites D. Chutia’s original edition of 1989–90.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"This is an edition locator, not a new reading. Identification with Sri Jivara and an eighth-century date are proposals. It is kept outside the securely dated 350–1100 core pending the full edition.","source_url":"https://abhidhajournal.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/Mrigakhee-Saikia.pdf","record_id":"B07","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Regional / local inscription","scope_350_1100":"overlaps_uncertain","evidence_status":"Published survey checked","source_locator":"Mrigakhee Saikia (2021), Archaeological Remains of the Kapili-Jamuna Valley of Assam, pp.38–39,44; D. Chutia, Journal of Assam Research Society XXXI (1 & 2), 1989–90, pp.106–114","summary_file":"Supplementary inscriptions/B07-Palasini-fragmentary-copper-plate-of-Jivaraja.txt","coordinate_type":"regional_reference","coordinate_reference":"Nagaon city, reference for historical Nagaon region","location_source":"https://www.pcbassam.org/SOURCE_STUDIES/NAGAON_NCAP_FINAL_REPORT_MARCH_2025.pdf","location_notes":"Broad regional reference only. The exact modern village has not been matched; proximity to Nagaon city is not established.","summary_text":"Palasini fragmentary copper plate of Jivaraja\n\nDate: Probably early medieval; identification with eighth-century Jivara proposed\nRuler or named person: Maharajadhiraja Sri Jivaraja\nPlace: Palasini, Nagaon\n\nSUMMARY\nThe published archaeological discussion identifies a fragmentary land-grant plate naming Sri Jivaraja and a Brahmin from Kamarupa. It cites D. Chutia’s original edition of 1989–90.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nAdds a grant outside Sharma’s formal corpus and a possible connection between central Assam and the Deopani image records.\n\nLIMITS OF THE EVIDENCE\nThis is an edition locator, not a new reading. Identification with Sri Jivara and an eighth-century date are proposals. It is kept outside the securely dated 350–1100 core pending the full edition.\n\nSource: Mrigakhee Saikia (2021), Archaeological Remains of the Kapili-Jamuna Valley of Assam, pp.38–39,44; D. Chutia, Journal of Assam Research Society XXXI (1 & 2), 1989–90, pp.106–114\nSource location: https://abhidhajournal.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/Mrigakhee-Saikia.pdf\nEvidence checked: Published survey checked\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/Supplementary inscriptions/B07-Palasini-fragmentary-copper-plate-of-Jivaraja.txt","source_href":"https://abhidhajournal.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/Mrigakhee-Saikia.pdf"},{"list_order":"16","inscription_name":"Deopani Vishnu Image Inscription","date_display":"Eighth or ninth century CE; palaeographic datings differ.","present_place":"Deopani, Assam, India","latitude":26.219709892356004,"longitude":93.82396043236585,"coordinate_precision":"User-supplied locality point; accuracy not independently assessed","date_start_ce":700.0,"date_end_ce":899.0,"material":"Inscribed stone image","king_or_named_person":"No securely identified issuing ruler in this record.","findspot":"Deopani; a standing stone Vishnu image discussed as preserved in the Assam State Museum.","event_or_recorded_subject":"Four lines on the back of a Vishnu image contain a difficult religious text. Sharma tentatively interprets it as invoking Shiva, discussing the Goddess in relation to Vishnu, referring to worship by different social groups and identifying the image with Narayana. Part of the right-hand edge is lost.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE. Palaeographic date or reading is disputed; see summary.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"SA02","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 360-363","summary_file":"A02-Deopani-Vishnu-Image-Inscription.txt","coordinate_type":"user_supplied_locality","coordinate_reference":"Deopani — user-identified locality","location_source":"User-provided coordinates in this conversation, 1 November 2026","location_notes":"Coordinates supplied by the user for the modern locality. This does not independently establish the exact ancient recovery point or the current repository. Decimal precision is retained as provided and is not a statement of positional accuracy.","summary_text":"Deopani Vishnu Image Inscription\n\nDate: Eighth or ninth century CE; palaeographic datings differ.\nRuler: No securely identified issuing ruler in this record.\nPlace: Deopani; a standing stone Vishnu image discussed as preserved in the Assam State Museum.\n\nSUMMARY\nFour lines on the back of a Vishnu image contain a difficult religious text. Sharma tentatively interprets it as invoking Shiva, discussing the Goddess in relation to Vishnu, referring to worship by different social groups and identifying the image with Narayana. Part of the right-hand edge is lost.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nEvidence for interaction among Shaiva, Vaishnava and Goddess traditions. The reading and interpretation are tentative, so detailed doctrinal conclusions should be treated cautiously.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 360-363.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/A02-Deopani-Vishnu-Image-Inscription.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"17","inscription_name":"Pashupati temple inscription of Jayadeva II","date_display":"737 CE; correction noted by Sharma","present_place":"Pashupatinath temple precinct, Deopatan, Kathmandu, Nepal","latitude":27.70972,"longitude":85.34861,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate temple-precinct location","date_start_ce":737.0,"date_end_ce":737.0,"material":"Stone","king_or_named_person":"Jayadeva II; Rajyamati and Harshadeva","findspot":"Kathmandu, Nepal","event_or_recorded_subject":"Records Jayadeva’s marriage to Rajyamati, daughter of Harshadeva, and describes her family through Bhagadatta ancestry.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Harshadeva’s identification with the Kamarupa ruler connects the inscription to Assam. This is not a local inscription.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"E04","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"External reference / identification","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Introduction notice image checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.15–0.16; supplied PDF p. 32","summary_file":"External inscriptions/E04-Pashupati-temple-inscription-of-Jayadeva-II.txt","coordinate_type":"temple_precinct","coordinate_reference":"Pashupatinath temple precinct","location_source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pashupatinath_Temple","location_notes":"Temple coordinate; the individual inscription position is not separately surveyed here.","summary_text":"Pashupati temple inscription of Jayadeva II\n\nDate: 737 CE; correction noted by Sharma\nRuler or persons named: Jayadeva II; Rajyamati and Harshadeva\nPlace: Kathmandu, Nepal\n\nSUMMARY\nRecords Jayadeva’s marriage to Rajyamati, daughter of Harshadeva, and describes her family through Bhagadatta ancestry.\n\nHOW IT RELATES TO KAMARUPA\nHarshadeva’s identification with the Kamarupa ruler connects the inscription to Assam. This is not a local inscription.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.15–0.16, supplied PDF p. 32. This summary follows Sharma’s notice; the full external edition has not been re-edited here.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/External inscriptions/E04-Pashupati-temple-inscription-of-Jayadeva-II.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"18","inscription_name":"Samangad plates of Dantidurga","date_display":"Mid-eighth century CE; exact conversion not re-edited here","present_place":"Samangad, Gadhinglaj area, Kolhapur district, Maharashtra, India","latitude":16.175716,"longitude":74.405158,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate fort reference","date_start_ce":750.0,"date_end_ce":759.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Dantidurga; Kirtivarman and Sri Harsha named","findspot":"Samangad, Maharashtra","event_or_recorded_subject":"A victory eulogy mentions Sri Harsha among opponents in a passage concerning the Karnataka ruler.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Sharma proposes an identification with a Kamarupa ruler; the identification is not explicit in the inscription.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"E05","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"External reference / identification","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Introduction notice image checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.16; supplied PDF p. 33","summary_file":"External inscriptions/E05-Samangad-plates-of-Dantidurga.txt","coordinate_type":"fort","coordinate_reference":"Samangad fort","location_source":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q7408585","location_notes":"Fort coordinate, not the precise copper-plate recovery position.","summary_text":"Samangad plates of Dantidurga\n\nDate: Mid-eighth century CE; exact conversion not re-edited here\nRuler or persons named: Dantidurga; Kirtivarman and Sri Harsha named\nPlace: Samangad, Maharashtra\n\nSUMMARY\nA victory eulogy mentions Sri Harsha among opponents in a passage concerning the Karnataka ruler.\n\nHOW IT RELATES TO KAMARUPA\nSharma proposes an identification with a Kamarupa ruler; the identification is not explicit in the inscription.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.16, supplied PDF p. 33. This summary follows Sharma’s notice; the full external edition has not been re-edited here.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/External inscriptions/E05-Samangad-plates-of-Dantidurga.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"19","inscription_name":"Kaliabor / Dighaligaon copper plates","date_display":"Ninth century CE","present_place":"Dighaligaon / Dighali, Kaliabor area, Nagaon district, Assam, India","latitude":26.55,"longitude":92.929,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — broad regional reference","date_start_ce":800.0,"date_end_ce":899.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Vanamalavarmadeva","findspot":"Dighaligaon, Kaliabor, Nagaon","event_or_recorded_subject":"The museum record describes three inscribed plates bound by a ring and elephant seal. It identifies the donor as Vanamalavarmadeva, son and successor of Harjaravarman, and records a gift of a khandalaka of land in Purjeka-pardisa in the southern division.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"NMMA’s descriptive paragraph is used; its structured dynasty field incorrectly says Late Ahom Period. No exact CE grant year is asserted here.","source_url":"https://nmma.nic.in/nmma/antiqDetail.do?object=98&refId=216028","record_id":"B02","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Regional / local inscription","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Official record checked","source_locator":"NMMA, Assam State Museum record SM-ASM-AM-216028; Chutia edition identified in Saikia 2021, p.44","summary_file":"Supplementary inscriptions/B02-Kaliabor-Dighaligaon-copper-plates.txt","coordinate_type":"regional_landmark","coordinate_reference":"Kaliabor College / Kuwaritol locality","location_source":"https://www.kaliaborcollege.ac.in/upload/dvv/1703939905.pdf","location_notes":"Identifiable landmark in the Kaliabor region. It is not Dighaligaon village or the charter discovery plot.","summary_text":"Kaliabor / Dighaligaon copper plates\n\nDate: Ninth century CE\nRuler or named person: Vanamalavarmadeva\nPlace: Dighaligaon, Kaliabor, Nagaon\n\nSUMMARY\nThe museum record describes three inscribed plates bound by a ring and elephant seal. It identifies the donor as Vanamalavarmadeva, son and successor of Harjaravarman, and records a gift of a khandalaka of land in Purjeka-pardisa in the southern division.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nA separate Vanamala charter, additional to Tezpur and Parbatiya. The original edition is D. Chutia, Journal of Assam Research Society XXIX (1986–87), pp. 37–52.\n\nLIMITS OF THE EVIDENCE\nNMMA’s descriptive paragraph is used; its structured dynasty field incorrectly says Late Ahom Period. No exact CE grant year is asserted here.\n\nSource: NMMA, Assam State Museum record SM-ASM-AM-216028; Chutia edition identified in Saikia 2021, p.44\nSource location: https://nmma.nic.in/nmma/antiqDetail.do?object=98&refId=216028\nEvidence checked: Official record checked\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/Supplementary inscriptions/B02-Kaliabor-Dighaligaon-copper-plates.txt","source_href":"https://nmma.nic.in/nmma/antiqDetail.do?object=98&refId=216028"},{"list_order":"20","inscription_name":"Kaki Surya image inscription","date_display":"Ninth century CE in published inventories","present_place":"Kaki area, historical Nagaon region, Assam, India; precise modern village match unresolved","latitude":25.92,"longitude":92.95,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — broad regional reference","date_start_ce":800.0,"date_end_ce":899.0,"material":"Inscribed image","king_or_named_person":"Issuer not established","findspot":"Kaki, Nagaon","event_or_recorded_subject":"An inscribed Surya image from Kaki adds an image record to the regional corpus. A dedicated edition is attributed to D. Sarma under the title Inscription on the Surya image from Kaki.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Bibliographic summary only: the complete edition and legible inscription image are still needed. No donor, ruler or grant terms are inferred. The museum has a Surya record from Kaki, but this alone does not establish that it is the same inscribed object.","source_url":"https://ir.nbu.ac.in/bitstreams/3c252121-0da3-4453-b938-96da22aad23e/download","record_id":"B04","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Regional / local inscription","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Bibliography checked","source_locator":"Kings and Cults in the Land of Kamarupa, notes citing D. Sarma, pp.179–180; compare NMMA object SM-ASM-AM-167411","summary_file":"Supplementary inscriptions/B04-Kaki-Surya-image-inscription.txt","coordinate_type":"regional_reference","coordinate_reference":"Lanka town, Hojai region","location_source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lanka_(town)","location_notes":"Regional town reference for the Kaki area. The inscribed image recovery point has not been established.","summary_text":"Kaki Surya image inscription\n\nDate: Ninth century CE in published inventories\nRuler or named person: Issuer not established\nPlace: Kaki, Nagaon\n\nSUMMARY\nAn inscribed Surya image from Kaki adds an image record to the regional corpus. A dedicated edition is attributed to D. Sarma under the title Inscription on the Surya image from Kaki.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nA source for the material history of solar worship in central Assam.\n\nLIMITS OF THE EVIDENCE\nBibliographic summary only: the complete edition and legible inscription image are still needed. No donor, ruler or grant terms are inferred. The museum has a Surya record from Kaki, but this alone does not establish that it is the same inscribed object.\n\nSource: Kings and Cults in the Land of Kamarupa, notes citing D. Sarma, pp.179–180; compare NMMA object SM-ASM-AM-167411\nSource location: https://ir.nbu.ac.in/bitstreams/3c252121-0da3-4453-b938-96da22aad23e/download\nEvidence checked: Bibliography checked\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/Supplementary inscriptions/B04-Kaki-Surya-image-inscription.txt","source_href":"https://ir.nbu.ac.in/bitstreams/3c252121-0da3-4453-b938-96da22aad23e/download"},{"list_order":"21","inscription_name":"Kuruvabahi copper plates","date_display":"Ninth century CE, reign of Harjaravarman","present_place":"Kuruvabahi, historical Nagaon region, Assam, India; modern village match unresolved","latitude":26.35,"longitude":92.67,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — broad regional reference","date_start_ce":800.0,"date_end_ce":899.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Harjaravarman","findspot":"Kuruvabahi, Nagaon region","event_or_recorded_subject":"A separate charter of Harjaravarman is identified in the scholarly inventory. Its first edition is cited as D. Chutia, Kuruvabahi (Nagaon) Copper Plate Grant of Harjjara(varma)deva, Journal of Assam Research Society, 1981–82.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"The original edition and image were not accessible in this audit. Detailed beneficiary and boundary readings are not supplied.","source_url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/361527651_Assam_from_the_Fifth_to_the_Thirteenth_Centuries","record_id":"B06","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Regional / local inscription","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Bibliography checked","source_locator":"Rajat Sanyal, Assam from the Fifth to the Thirteenth Centuries (2014), p.217, item 4; original Chutia edition cited there","summary_file":"Supplementary inscriptions/B06-Kuruvabahi-copper-plates.txt","coordinate_type":"regional_reference","coordinate_reference":"Nagaon city, reference for historical Nagaon region","location_source":"https://www.pcbassam.org/SOURCE_STUDIES/NAGAON_NCAP_FINAL_REPORT_MARCH_2025.pdf","location_notes":"Broad regional reference only. The exact modern village has not been matched; proximity to Nagaon city is not established.","summary_text":"Kuruvabahi copper plates\n\nDate: Ninth century CE, reign of Harjaravarman\nRuler or named person: Harjaravarman\nPlace: Kuruvabahi, Nagaon region\n\nSUMMARY\nA separate charter of Harjaravarman is identified in the scholarly inventory. Its first edition is cited as D. Chutia, Kuruvabahi (Nagaon) Copper Plate Grant of Harjjara(varma)deva, Journal of Assam Research Society, 1981–82.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nAn additional charter absent from the 1978 formal corpus, distinct from Hayunthal.\n\nLIMITS OF THE EVIDENCE\nThe original edition and image were not accessible in this audit. Detailed beneficiary and boundary readings are not supplied.\n\nSource: Rajat Sanyal, Assam from the Fifth to the Thirteenth Centuries (2014), p.217, item 4; original Chutia edition cited there\nSource location: https://www.researchgate.net/publication/361527651_Assam_from_the_Fifth_to_the_Thirteenth_Centuries\nEvidence checked: Bibliography checked\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/Supplementary inscriptions/B06-Kuruvabahi-copper-plates.txt","source_href":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/361527651_Assam_from_the_Fifth_to_the_Thirteenth_Centuries"},{"list_order":"22","inscription_name":"Hayunthal Copper Plates Harjaravarman","date_display":"Middle of the ninth century CE.","present_place":"Hayunthal / Hayungthal, historical Mikir Hills region, Assam, India; precise modern village match unresolved","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"coordinate_precision":"Not georeferenced","date_start_ce":800.0,"date_end_ce":899.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Harjaravarman, Salastambha line.","findspot":"Hayunthal, described in the book as formerly in Nowgong and subsequently in the Mikir Hills district.","event_or_recorded_subject":"The surviving copper-plate material contains royal genealogy and praise connected with Harjaravarman. It recounts rulers of the Salastambha line and a narrative explaining their association with the term Mleccha. The document is incomplete, restricting recovery of the practical grant details.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S08","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 143-148","summary_file":"08-Hayunthal-Copper-Plates-Harjaravarman.txt","coordinate_type":"unresolved","coordinate_reference":"","location_source":"/home/angsuman/extra_spac/Primary source/Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","location_notes":"The broad locality is known, but a unique modern recovery point has not been verified. Coordinates intentionally blank.","summary_text":"Hayunthal Copper Plates Harjaravarman\n\nDate: Middle of the ninth century CE.\nRuler: Harjaravarman, Salastambha line.\nPlace: Hayunthal, described in the book as formerly in Nowgong and subsequently in the Mikir Hills district.\n\nSUMMARY\nThe surviving copper-plate material contains royal genealogy and praise connected with Harjaravarman. It recounts rulers of the Salastambha line and a narrative explaining their association with the term Mleccha. The document is incomplete, restricting recovery of the practical grant details.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nAn important source for reconstructing the early Salastambha succession and the dynasty’s explanation of its origins. Its genealogical praise needs to be distinguished from independently established chronology.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 143-148.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/08-Hayunthal-Copper-Plates-Harjaravarman.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"23","inscription_name":"Tezpur Copper Plates Vanamala","date_display":"Middle of the ninth century CE.","present_place":"Tezpur area, Sonitpur district, Assam, India","latitude":26.63,"longitude":92.8,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate city reference","date_start_ce":800.0,"date_end_ce":899.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Vanamala or Vanamalavarmadeva, Salastambha line.","findspot":"Found near Tezpur.","event_or_recorded_subject":"A royal copper-plate charter combines genealogical praise with a religious land donation. Its description of Haruppesvara evokes a prosperous royal centre and its Brahmaputra setting. Much of its formal text parallels Vanamala’s Parbatiya charter. Sharma relies on earlier published readings because the original plates were unavailable to him.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S09","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 149-167","summary_file":"09-Tezpur-Copper-Plates-Vanamala.txt","coordinate_type":"city","coordinate_reference":"Tezpur city","location_source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tezpur","location_notes":"City reference only; exact copper-plate findspot is not established.","summary_text":"Tezpur Copper Plates Vanamala\n\nDate: Middle of the ninth century CE.\nRuler: Vanamala or Vanamalavarmadeva, Salastambha line.\nPlace: Found near Tezpur.\n\nSUMMARY\nA royal copper-plate charter combines genealogical praise with a religious land donation. Its description of Haruppesvara evokes a prosperous royal centre and its Brahmaputra setting. Much of its formal text parallels Vanamala’s Parbatiya charter. Sharma relies on earlier published readings because the original plates were unavailable to him.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nUseful for the dynasty’s genealogy, royal capital, religious patronage and the landscape of the Tezpur region. Its transmission through early copies means some readings require caution.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 149-167.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/09-Tezpur-Copper-Plates-Vanamala.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"24","inscription_name":"Parbatiya Copper Plates Vanamalavarmadeva","date_display":"Middle of the ninth century CE.","present_place":"Parbatiya village, Tezpur area, Sonitpur district, Assam, India","latitude":26.63,"longitude":92.8,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — broad regional reference","date_start_ce":800.0,"date_end_ce":899.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Vanamalavarmadeva, Salastambha line.","findspot":"Parbatiya, near Tezpur.","event_or_recorded_subject":"The charter grants the village of Haposu to a Brahmin and records the beneficiary’s family, the royal genealogy and the religious merit sought for the king and his parents. It also describes the royal centre of Haruppesvara and its setting. The text has substantial parallels with the Tezpur plates of the same king.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S10","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 168-180","summary_file":"10-Parbatiya-Copper-Plates-Vanamalavarmadeva.txt","coordinate_type":"regional_reference","coordinate_reference":"Tezpur city","location_source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tezpur","location_notes":"Tezpur-area reference only. It does not locate the particular Parbatiya or Naharhabi/Bargaon recovery plot.","summary_text":"Parbatiya Copper Plates Vanamalavarmadeva\n\nDate: Middle of the ninth century CE.\nRuler: Vanamalavarmadeva, Salastambha line.\nPlace: Parbatiya, near Tezpur.\n\nSUMMARY\nThe charter grants the village of Haposu to a Brahmin and records the beneficiary’s family, the royal genealogy and the religious merit sought for the king and his parents. It also describes the royal centre of Haruppesvara and its setting. The text has substantial parallels with the Tezpur plates of the same king.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nProvides evidence for land donation, Brahmin patronage and the royal centre in the Tezpur region. Comparing the two Vanamala charters helps identify shared formulas and differences specific to each transaction.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 168-180.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/10-Parbatiya-Copper-Plates-Vanamalavarmadeva.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"25","inscription_name":"Tezpur Rock Inscription Harjaravarman","date_display":"Gupta year 510, conventionally about 829–830 CE.","present_place":"Brahmaputra bank near Tezpur, Sonitpur district, Assam, India","latitude":26.616667,"longitude":92.783333,"coordinate_precision":"Published ASI locality coordinate; minute precision","date_start_ce":829.0,"date_end_ce":830.0,"material":"Rock / stone","king_or_named_person":"Harjaravarman, Salastambha line.","findspot":"A rock beside the Brahmaputra at Tezpur.","event_or_recorded_subject":"A damaged public record concerns a dispute and regulations affecting boats, boatmen or fishermen and officials. Earlier scholars interpreted it as a judicial settlement involving land; Sharma questions that reading and understands its central purpose as regulating where civilian boats could operate, with a payment for infringement.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S07","regnal_or_era_date":"Gupta year 510","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 136-142","summary_file":"07-Tezpur-Rock-Inscription-Harjaravarman.txt","coordinate_type":"monument_locality","coordinate_reference":"Tezpur rock-inscription locality","location_source":"https://www.asiguwahaticircle.gov.in/rock%20inscription.html","location_notes":"ASI publishes coordinates rounded to minutes; do not treat the decimal conversion as a precise GPS survey.","summary_text":"Tezpur Rock Inscription Harjaravarman\n\nDate: Gupta year 510, conventionally about 829–830 CE.\nRuler: Harjaravarman, Salastambha line.\nPlace: A rock beside the Brahmaputra at Tezpur.\n\nSUMMARY\nA damaged public record concerns a dispute and regulations affecting boats, boatmen or fishermen and officials. Earlier scholars interpreted it as a judicial settlement involving land; Sharma questions that reading and understands its central purpose as regulating where civilian boats could operate, with a payment for infringement.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nRare evidence for river administration, official authority and economic life. It is valuable precisely because it differs from the usual religious land charter. The damaged text makes the detailed legal interpretation uncertain.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 136-142.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/07-Tezpur-Rock-Inscription-Harjaravarman.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"26","inscription_name":"Uttarbarbil Howraghat Copper Plates Balavarman III","date_display":"Fifth regnal year of Balavarman III; late ninth century CE.","present_place":"Uttarbarbil / Uttar Borbil, Howraghat area, Karbi Anglong district, Assam, India","latitude":26.087,"longitude":93.138,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — nearby reference, not exact findspot","date_start_ce":850.0,"date_end_ce":899.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Balavarman III, Salastambha line.","findspot":"Uttarbarbil in the Howraghat area; also called the Howraghat plates.","event_or_recorded_subject":"A copper-plate grant records a donation to a Brahmin, with royal genealogy, praise, the beneficiary’s background, the occasion of the gift and the boundaries of the donated land. Its wording closely resembles the Nowgong charter.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S11","regnal_or_era_date":"Regnal year 5","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 181-195","summary_file":"11-Uttarbarbil-Howraghat-Copper-Plates-Balavarman-III.txt","coordinate_type":"nearby_town","coordinate_reference":"Howraghat town","location_source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Howraghat","location_notes":"Nearby town reference for Uttarbarbil / Howraghat; not the original copper-plate digging point.","summary_text":"Uttarbarbil Howraghat Copper Plates Balavarman III\n\nDate: Fifth regnal year of Balavarman III; late ninth century CE.\nRuler: Balavarman III, Salastambha line.\nPlace: Uttarbarbil in the Howraghat area; also called the Howraghat plates.\n\nSUMMARY\nA copper-plate grant records a donation to a Brahmin, with royal genealogy, praise, the beneficiary’s background, the occasion of the gift and the boundaries of the donated land. Its wording closely resembles the Nowgong charter.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nDocuments Balavarman III’s authority and religious patronage. Uttarbarbil and Howraghat are alternative names for this record, not two separate grants. Its regnal year can be compared with his eighth-year and thirteenth-year charters.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 181-195.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/11-Uttarbarbil-Howraghat-Copper-Plates-Balavarman-III.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"27","inscription_name":"Nowgong Copper Plates Balavarman III","date_display":"Eighth regnal year of Balavarman III; late ninth century CE.","present_place":"Nagaon region, Assam, India; exact original recovery point not georeferenced","latitude":26.35,"longitude":92.67,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — nearby reference, not exact findspot","date_start_ce":850.0,"date_end_ce":899.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Balavarman III, Salastambha line.","findspot":"Nowgong, now generally spelled Nagaon.","event_or_recorded_subject":"This charter records the donation of land called Hamsava in the Dijjina district on the southern bank, assessed as yielding 4,000 units of paddy. It describes the beneficiary, informs relevant officials and residents, and marks boundaries using roads, trees and ponds. Its genealogy and praise largely parallel the Uttarbarbil charter.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S12","regnal_or_era_date":"Regnal year 8","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 196-205","summary_file":"12-Nowgong-Copper-Plates-Balavarman-III.txt","coordinate_type":"nearby_city","coordinate_reference":"Nagaon city","location_source":"https://www.pcbassam.org/SOURCE_STUDIES/NAGAON_NCAP_FINAL_REPORT_MARCH_2025.pdf","location_notes":"City-level reference for the Nagaon/Nowgong provenance, not the precise copper-plate recovery point.","summary_text":"Nowgong Copper Plates Balavarman III\n\nDate: Eighth regnal year of Balavarman III; late ninth century CE.\nRuler: Balavarman III, Salastambha line.\nPlace: Nowgong, now generally spelled Nagaon.\n\nSUMMARY\nThis charter records the donation of land called Hamsava in the Dijjina district on the southern bank, assessed as yielding 4,000 units of paddy. It describes the beneficiary, informs relevant officials and residents, and marks boundaries using roads, trees and ponds. Its genealogy and praise largely parallel the Uttarbarbil charter.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nUseful for land assessment, administrative geography and the practical recording of boundaries. Together with the king’s other grants, it helps establish the relative sequence of his reign.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 196-205.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/12-Nowgong-Copper-Plates-Balavarman-III.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"28","inscription_name":"Ulubari Copper Plates Balavarman III","date_display":"Thirteenth regnal year of Balavarman III; late ninth century CE.","present_place":"Ulubari, Naharbari mauza, historical Darrang region, Assam, India; modern village match unresolved","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"coordinate_precision":"Not georeferenced","date_start_ce":850.0,"date_end_ce":899.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Balavarman III, Salastambha line.","findspot":"Ulubari village in Naharbari mauza, Darrang region; discovered in 1977.","event_or_recorded_subject":"A three-plate charter grants land to Bhayadeva, son of Budhadeva and grandson of Vishnudeva. Its royal seal and much of its formal text resemble the Uttarbarbil and Nowgong grants. Sharma incorporates the newly discovered charter in the appendix and discusses its readings and grant formula.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"SA05","regnal_or_era_date":"Regnal year 13","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 367-371","summary_file":"A05-Ulubari-Copper-Plates-Balavarman-III.txt","coordinate_type":"unresolved","coordinate_reference":"","location_source":"/home/angsuman/extra_spac/Primary source/Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","location_notes":"The broad locality is known, but a unique modern recovery point has not been verified. Coordinates intentionally blank.","summary_text":"Ulubari Copper Plates Balavarman III\n\nDate: Thirteenth regnal year of Balavarman III; late ninth century CE.\nRuler: Balavarman III, Salastambha line.\nPlace: Ulubari village in Naharbari mauza, Darrang region; discovered in 1977.\n\nSUMMARY\nA three-plate charter grants land to Bhayadeva, son of Budhadeva and grandson of Vishnudeva. Its royal seal and much of its formal text resemble the Uttarbarbil and Nowgong grants. Sharma incorporates the newly discovered charter in the appendix and discusses its readings and grant formula.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nExtends the documented regnal sequence of Balavarman III from the fifth and eighth years to the thirteenth. It supplies additional evidence for Brahmin patronage, land-grant practice and the dynasty’s documentary conventions.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 367-371.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/A05-Ulubari-Copper-Plates-Balavarman-III.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"29","inscription_name":"Bhagalpur grant of Narayanapala","date_display":"Late ninth or early tenth century CE","present_place":"Bhagalpur, Bihar, India; precise discovery point not georeferenced","latitude":25.24,"longitude":86.97,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate city reference","date_start_ce":850.0,"date_end_ce":949.0,"material":"Copper plate","king_or_named_person":"Narayanapala; Devapala referenced","findspot":"Bhagalpur, Bihar","event_or_recorded_subject":"Contains a dynastic account used to discuss relations between Bengal’s Pala rulers and Kamarupa.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Narrated events may precede the issue of the grant. Bengal Palas and Kamarupa Palas are distinct dynasties.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"E06","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"External reference / identification","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Introduction notice image checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.16; supplied PDF p. 33","summary_file":"External inscriptions/E06-Bhagalpur-grant-of-Narayanapala.txt","coordinate_type":"city","coordinate_reference":"Bhagalpur city","location_source":"https://dateandtime.info/citycoordinates.php?id=1276300","location_notes":"City-level reference only; the original grant recovery point is not established.","summary_text":"Bhagalpur grant of Narayanapala\n\nDate: Late ninth or early tenth century CE\nRuler or persons named: Narayanapala; Devapala referenced\nPlace: Bhagalpur, Bihar\n\nSUMMARY\nContains a dynastic account used to discuss relations between Bengal’s Pala rulers and Kamarupa.\n\nHOW IT RELATES TO KAMARUPA\nNarrated events may precede the issue of the grant. Bengal Palas and Kamarupa Palas are distinct dynasties.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.16, supplied PDF p. 33. This summary follows Sharma’s notice; the full external edition has not been re-edited here.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/External inscriptions/E06-Bhagalpur-grant-of-Narayanapala.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"30","inscription_name":"Paschimbhag copper plates","date_display":"Tenth century CE","present_place":"Paschimbhag, Rajnagar upazila, Moulvibazar district, Sylhet Division, Bangladesh","latitude":24.565,"longitude":91.864,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — broad regional reference","date_start_ce":900.0,"date_end_ce":999.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Srichandra","findspot":"Paschimbhag, Sylhet region, Bangladesh","event_or_recorded_subject":"The eulogy claims a Kamarupa campaign and mentions Lauhitya and Pushpabhadra.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"An external campaign claim must not be converted automatically into a permanent frontier.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"E08","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"External reference / identification","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Introduction notice image checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.16; supplied PDF p. 33","summary_file":"External inscriptions/E08-Paschimbhag-copper-plates.txt","coordinate_type":"regional_reference","coordinate_reference":"Rajnagar upazila reference, Moulvibazar","location_source":"https://openadmindata.org/bd/upazilas/","location_notes":"Upazila-level reference for Paschimbhag; not the pond or exact village recovery point.","summary_text":"Paschimbhag copper plates\n\nDate: Tenth century CE\nRuler or persons named: Srichandra\nPlace: Paschimbhag, Sylhet region, Bangladesh\n\nSUMMARY\nThe eulogy claims a Kamarupa campaign and mentions Lauhitya and Pushpabhadra.\n\nHOW IT RELATES TO KAMARUPA\nAn external campaign claim must not be converted automatically into a permanent frontier.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.16, supplied PDF p. 33. This summary follows Sharma’s notice; the full external edition has not been re-edited here.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/External inscriptions/E08-Paschimbhag-copper-plates.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"31","inscription_name":"Grant of Ganga king Vajravarman cited by Sharma","date_display":"922 CE as cited by Sharma","present_place":"Odisha region, India; original grant and exact modern findspot not securely identified","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"coordinate_precision":"Not georeferenced","date_start_ce":922.0,"date_end_ce":922.0,"material":"Copper plate","king_or_named_person":"Vajravarman in Sharma’s notice; exact royal-name reading requires checking","findspot":"Ganga kingdom, Odisha region; exact findspot not verified","event_or_recorded_subject":"Sharma reports a grant to Vishnumurti Acharya of Kamarupa.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Bibliographic notice only. Collect Journal of the Andhra Historical Research Society I, p.271, and Journal of Assam Research Society III, pp.113–115 before asserting the exact designation.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"E07","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"External reference / identification","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Introduction notice image checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.16; supplied PDF p. 33","summary_file":"External inscriptions/E07-Grant-of-Ganga-king-Vajravarman-cited-by-Sharma.txt","coordinate_type":"unresolved","coordinate_reference":"","location_source":"/home/angsuman/extra_spac/Primary source/Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","location_notes":"The broad locality is known, but a unique modern recovery point has not been verified. Coordinates intentionally blank.","summary_text":"Grant of Ganga king Vajravarman cited by Sharma\n\nDate: 922 CE as cited by Sharma\nRuler or persons named: Vajravarman in Sharma’s notice; exact royal-name reading requires checking\nPlace: Ganga kingdom, Odisha region; exact findspot not verified\n\nSUMMARY\nSharma reports a grant to Vishnumurti Acharya of Kamarupa.\n\nHOW IT RELATES TO KAMARUPA\nBibliographic notice only. Collect Journal of the Andhra Historical Research Society I, p.271, and Journal of Assam Research Society III, pp.113–115 before asserting the exact designation.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.16, supplied PDF p. 33. This summary follows Sharma’s notice; the full external edition has not been re-edited here.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/External inscriptions/E07-Grant-of-Ganga-king-Vajravarman-cited-by-Sharma.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"32","inscription_name":"Coratbari copper plates","date_display":"Eleventh century CE; twelfth regnal year of Ratnapala","present_place":"Coratbari, historical Nagaon region, Assam, India; exact modern identification unresolved","latitude":26.35,"longitude":92.67,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — broad regional reference","date_start_ce":1000.0,"date_end_ce":1099.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Ratnapala","findspot":"Conventionally Coratbari; Sharma could not establish how and when the plates entered the Assam State Museum.","event_or_recorded_subject":"A three-plate grant gives land yielding 4,000 units of paddy, taken from Santidasa Pataka and Bhattaya Pataka in Havrnga visaya, to Siddhapala, son of Nidhipala and Dattayika and grandson of Jayapala. Sharma reads a city-name Hadapyaka and explicitly rejects an earlier report that the readable text named Hadapesvara.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"A damaged record, presented only partly in the introduction. Twelfth regnal year is checked in the page image. The old CSV’s exact c.1021 date is withdrawn; accession chronology is uncertain. Some place-name spellings remain uncertain.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"B01","regnal_or_era_date":"Regnal year 12","relation_to_kamarupa":"Regional / local inscription","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition image checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; introduction pp. 0.21–0.24, supplied PDF pp. 38–41","summary_file":"Supplementary inscriptions/B01-Coratbari-copper-plates.txt","coordinate_type":"regional_reference","coordinate_reference":"Nagaon city, reference for historical Nagaon region","location_source":"https://www.pcbassam.org/SOURCE_STUDIES/NAGAON_NCAP_FINAL_REPORT_MARCH_2025.pdf","location_notes":"Broad regional reference only. The exact modern village has not been matched; proximity to Nagaon city is not established.","summary_text":"Coratbari copper plates\n\nDate: Eleventh century CE; twelfth regnal year of Ratnapala\nRuler or named person: Ratnapala\nPlace: Conventionally Coratbari; Sharma could not establish how and when the plates entered the Assam State Museum.\n\nSUMMARY\nA three-plate grant gives land yielding 4,000 units of paddy, taken from Santidasa Pataka and Bhattaya Pataka in Havrnga visaya, to Siddhapala, son of Nidhipala and Dattayika and grandson of Jayapala. Sharma reads a city-name Hadapyaka and explicitly rejects an earlier report that the readable text named Hadapesvara.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nAdds a distinct Ratnapala charter to the two in the main corpus. It contains evidence for a city, beneficiary genealogy, agricultural assessment and land boundaries.\n\nLIMITS OF THE EVIDENCE\nA damaged record, presented only partly in the introduction. Twelfth regnal year is checked in the page image. The old CSV’s exact c.1021 date is withdrawn; accession chronology is uncertain. Some place-name spellings remain uncertain.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; introduction pp. 0.21–0.24, supplied PDF pp. 38–41\nSource location: Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf\nEvidence checked: Edition image checked\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/Supplementary inscriptions/B01-Coratbari-copper-plates.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"33","inscription_name":"Mainamati plates of Ladahachandra","date_display":"Eleventh century CE","present_place":"Lalmai–Mainamati archaeological region, Cumilla, Chattogram Division, Bangladesh","latitude":23.496,"longitude":91.108,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — broad regional reference","date_start_ce":1000.0,"date_end_ce":1099.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Ladahachandra; Srichandra praised","findspot":"Mainamati, Bangladesh","event_or_recorded_subject":"Contains praise of Srichandra using the grief of women of Pragjyotisha as an image of military success.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"The poetic claim is evidence for royal representation and possible conflict, not a detailed campaign report.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"E14","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"External reference / identification","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Introduction notice image checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.18; supplied PDF p. 35","summary_file":"External inscriptions/E14-Mainamati-plates-of-Ladahachandra.txt","coordinate_type":"regional_landmark","coordinate_reference":"Ranir Bunglow / Rani Mainamatir Bari, Mainamati","location_source":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/6670/","location_notes":"Published landmark in the Mainamati archaeological cluster, used only as a regional reference. No claim that either charter was found at this particular monument.","summary_text":"Mainamati plates of Ladahachandra\n\nDate: Eleventh century CE\nRuler or persons named: Ladahachandra; Srichandra praised\nPlace: Mainamati, Bangladesh\n\nSUMMARY\nContains praise of Srichandra using the grief of women of Pragjyotisha as an image of military success.\n\nHOW IT RELATES TO KAMARUPA\nThe poetic claim is evidence for royal representation and possible conflict, not a detailed campaign report.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.18, supplied PDF p. 35. This summary follows Sharma’s notice; the full external edition has not been re-edited here.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/External inscriptions/E14-Mainamati-plates-of-Ladahachandra.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"34","inscription_name":"Mainamati plates of Govindachandra","date_display":"Eleventh century CE","present_place":"Lalmai–Mainamati archaeological region, Cumilla, Chattogram Division, Bangladesh","latitude":23.496,"longitude":91.108,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — broad regional reference","date_start_ce":1000.0,"date_end_ce":1099.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Govindachandra; Kalyanachandra praised","findspot":"Mainamati, Bangladesh","event_or_recorded_subject":"Praises Kalyanachandra through a passage about Lauhitya and the tears of Mleccha women.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"The implied association with Kamarupa and the historical scale of the campaign require interpretation.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"E15","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"External reference / identification","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Introduction notice image checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.18; supplied PDF p. 35","summary_file":"External inscriptions/E15-Mainamati-plates-of-Govindachandra.txt","coordinate_type":"regional_landmark","coordinate_reference":"Ranir Bunglow / Rani Mainamatir Bari, Mainamati","location_source":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/6670/","location_notes":"Published landmark in the Mainamati archaeological cluster, used only as a regional reference. No claim that either charter was found at this particular monument.","summary_text":"Mainamati plates of Govindachandra\n\nDate: Eleventh century CE\nRuler or persons named: Govindachandra; Kalyanachandra praised\nPlace: Mainamati, Bangladesh\n\nSUMMARY\nPraises Kalyanachandra through a passage about Lauhitya and the tears of Mleccha women.\n\nHOW IT RELATES TO KAMARUPA\nThe implied association with Kamarupa and the historical scale of the campaign require interpretation.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.18, supplied PDF p. 35. This summary follows Sharma’s notice; the full external edition has not been re-edited here.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/External inscriptions/E15-Mainamati-plates-of-Govindachandra.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"35","inscription_name":"Bargaon Copper Plates Ratnapala","date_display":"Twenty-fifth regnal year of Ratnapala; approximately 1035 CE in Sharma’s chronology.","present_place":"Naharhabi / Bargaon area, Tezpur region, Assam, India; precise recovery point unresolved","latitude":26.63,"longitude":92.8,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — broad regional reference","date_start_ce":1035.0,"date_end_ce":1035.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Ratnapala, Kamarupa Pala dynasty.","findspot":"Bargaon in the Tezpur region.","event_or_recorded_subject":"Ratnapala grants land capable of yielding 2,000 units of paddy to the Brahmin Viradatta. The land lies in Lavukuti within the district called Trayodasagrama on the northern bank of the Brahmaputra. The charter supplies royal genealogy, praise, grant conditions and detailed boundaries.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S13","regnal_or_era_date":"Regnal year 25","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 206-226","summary_file":"13-Bargaon-Copper-Plates-Ratnapala.txt","coordinate_type":"regional_reference","coordinate_reference":"Tezpur city","location_source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tezpur","location_notes":"Tezpur-area reference only. It does not locate the particular Parbatiya or Naharhabi/Bargaon recovery plot.","summary_text":"Bargaon Copper Plates Ratnapala\n\nDate: Twenty-fifth regnal year of Ratnapala; approximately 1035 CE in Sharma’s chronology.\nRuler: Ratnapala, Kamarupa Pala dynasty.\nPlace: Bargaon in the Tezpur region.\n\nSUMMARY\nRatnapala grants land capable of yielding 2,000 units of paddy to the Brahmin Viradatta. The land lies in Lavukuti within the district called Trayodasagrama on the northern bank of the Brahmaputra. The charter supplies royal genealogy, praise, grant conditions and detailed boundaries.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nA principal source for the Kamarupa Pala dynasty and Ratnapala’s administration. Its fiscal description and place names illuminate agricultural settlement, although several ancient localities cannot be securely identified.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 206-226.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/13-Bargaon-Copper-Plates-Ratnapala.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"36","inscription_name":"Suwalkuchi Copper Plates Ratnapala","date_display":"Twenty-sixth regnal year of Ratnapala; approximately 1036 CE as headed in this edition.","present_place":"Sualkuchi / Suwalkuchi, Kamrup district, Assam, India","latitude":26.16806,"longitude":91.57111,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate town reference","date_start_ce":1036.0,"date_end_ce":1036.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Ratnapala, Kamarupa Pala dynasty.","findspot":"Suwalkuchi or Sualkuchi, Kamrup.","event_or_recorded_subject":"The charter records a Brahmin land grant, with royal genealogy, the beneficiary’s ancestry, privileges and detailed boundaries. Boundary markers include trees, a brick field and neighbouring holdings. The twenty-sixth regnal year follows the Bargaon charter’s twenty-fifth year.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S14","regnal_or_era_date":"Regnal year 26","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 227-232","summary_file":"14-Suwalkuchi-Copper-Plates-Ratnapala.txt","coordinate_type":"town","coordinate_reference":"Sualkuchi / Soalkuchi","location_source":"https://www.geonames.org/1255910","location_notes":"Town coordinate only; not the original plot of land donated or the precise recovery point.","summary_text":"Suwalkuchi Copper Plates Ratnapala\n\nDate: Twenty-sixth regnal year of Ratnapala; approximately 1036 CE as headed in this edition.\nRuler: Ratnapala, Kamarupa Pala dynasty.\nPlace: Suwalkuchi or Sualkuchi, Kamrup.\n\nSUMMARY\nThe charter records a Brahmin land grant, with royal genealogy, the beneficiary’s ancestry, privileges and detailed boundaries. Boundary markers include trees, a brick field and neighbouring holdings. The twenty-sixth regnal year follows the Bargaon charter’s twenty-fifth year.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nUseful for the dynasty’s succession, rural geography and the documentary form of royal grants. Its approximate CE date depends on the accession chronology adopted.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 227-232.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/14-Suwalkuchi-Copper-Plates-Ratnapala.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"37","inscription_name":"Silimpur stone slab inscription","date_display":"Late eleventh or early twelfth century; exact placement not fixed here","present_place":"Silimpur, historical Bogra region, Bangladesh; exact modern village match unresolved","latitude":24.85,"longitude":89.37,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — broad regional reference","date_start_ce":1050.0,"date_end_ce":1149.0,"material":"Stone slab","king_or_named_person":"Jayapaladeva","findspot":"Silimpur, Bogra region, Bangladesh","event_or_recorded_subject":"Records a tulapurusha charitable offering associated with Jayapaladeva.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Jayapala’s political and chronological identification needs the original edition. Do not place it securely before 1100.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"E12","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"External reference / identification","scope_350_1100":"overlaps_uncertain","evidence_status":"Introduction notice image checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.17; supplied PDF p. 34","summary_file":"External inscriptions/E12-Silimpur-stone-slab-inscription.txt","coordinate_type":"regional_reference","coordinate_reference":"Bogra / Bogura city","location_source":"https://www.susana.org/_resources/documents/default/3-4965-7-1648844750.pdf","location_notes":"Broad historical Bogra-region reference only. Silimpur village has not been securely matched and need not be close to the city point.","summary_text":"Silimpur stone slab inscription\n\nDate: Late eleventh or early twelfth century; exact placement not fixed here\nRuler or persons named: Jayapaladeva\nPlace: Silimpur, Bogra region, Bangladesh\n\nSUMMARY\nRecords a tulapurusha charitable offering associated with Jayapaladeva.\n\nHOW IT RELATES TO KAMARUPA\nJayapala’s political and chronological identification needs the original edition. Do not place it securely before 1100.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.17, supplied PDF p. 34. This summary follows Sharma’s notice; the full external edition has not been re-edited here.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/External inscriptions/E12-Silimpur-stone-slab-inscription.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"38","inscription_name":"Gauhati Copper Plates Indrapala","date_display":"Eighth regnal year of Indrapala; approximately 1058 CE in Sharma’s chronology.","present_place":"Barpanara, Assam, India","latitude":26.418977850439497,"longitude":91.75189234914802,"coordinate_precision":"User-supplied locality point; accuracy not independently assessed","date_start_ce":1058.0,"date_end_ce":1058.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Indrapala, grandson and successor of Ratnapala in the Kamarupa Pala line.","findspot":"Barpanari in the Pati-Darrang area of the Guwahati subdivision, as described in the book.","event_or_recorded_subject":"The king grants a plot assessed as yielding 4,000 units of paddy to the Brahmin Dehapala. The land is located in Hapyoma visaya on the northern bank of the Brahmaputra. The charter includes genealogy, praise, privileges and boundaries. Its damaged readings can sometimes be checked against the Guwakuchi charter.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S15","regnal_or_era_date":"Regnal year 8","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 233-246","summary_file":"15-Gauhati-Copper-Plates-Indrapala.txt","coordinate_type":"user_supplied_locality","coordinate_reference":"Barpanara — user-identified locality","location_source":"User-provided coordinates in this conversation, 1 November 2026","location_notes":"Coordinates supplied by the user for the modern locality. This does not independently establish the exact ancient recovery point or the current repository. Decimal precision is retained as provided and is not a statement of positional accuracy.","summary_text":"Gauhati Copper Plates Indrapala\n\nDate: Eighth regnal year of Indrapala; approximately 1058 CE in Sharma’s chronology.\nRuler: Indrapala, grandson and successor of Ratnapala in the Kamarupa Pala line.\nPlace: Barpanari in the Pati-Darrang area of the Guwahati subdivision, as described in the book.\n\nSUMMARY\nThe king grants a plot assessed as yielding 4,000 units of paddy to the Brahmin Dehapala. The land is located in Hapyoma visaya on the northern bank of the Brahmaputra. The charter includes genealogy, praise, privileges and boundaries. Its damaged readings can sometimes be checked against the Guwakuchi charter.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nEvidence for Pala succession, Brahmin patronage, land assessment and northern-bank administration. The title Gauhati identifies the published grant and should not be mistaken for an exact findspot within the modern city.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 233-246.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/15-Gauhati-Copper-Plates-Indrapala.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"39","inscription_name":"Guwakuchi Copper Plates Indrapala","date_display":"Twenty-first regnal year of Indrapala; approximately 1071 CE in Sharma’s chronology.","present_place":"Guwakuchi / Guakuchi, Nalbari area, Assam, India; competing village matches need resolution","latitude":26.445,"longitude":91.44,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — nearby reference, not exact findspot","date_start_ce":1071.0,"date_end_ce":1071.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Indrapala, Kamarupa Pala dynasty.","findspot":"Guwakuchi near Nalbari; discovered in 1925.","event_or_recorded_subject":"The charter grants land yielding 2,000 units of paddy to Devadeva, a Brahmin whose family is traced through Vasudeva and Somadeva to a settlement in Sravasti. Much of the genealogy and formal language is shared with Indrapala’s Gauhati charter; the beneficiary and transaction details differ.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S16","regnal_or_era_date":"Regnal year 21","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 247-260","summary_file":"16-Guwakuchi-Copper-Plates-Indrapala.txt","coordinate_type":"nearby_town","coordinate_reference":"Nalbari town","location_source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nalbari","location_notes":"Town reference for Guwakuchi near Nalbari; avoids selecting one of the conflicting Guakuchi village coordinates.","summary_text":"Guwakuchi Copper Plates Indrapala\n\nDate: Twenty-first regnal year of Indrapala; approximately 1071 CE in Sharma’s chronology.\nRuler: Indrapala, Kamarupa Pala dynasty.\nPlace: Guwakuchi near Nalbari; discovered in 1925.\n\nSUMMARY\nThe charter grants land yielding 2,000 units of paddy to Devadeva, a Brahmin whose family is traced through Vasudeva and Somadeva to a settlement in Sravasti. Much of the genealogy and formal language is shared with Indrapala’s Gauhati charter; the beneficiary and transaction details differ.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nUseful for the king’s regnal sequence, the ancestry claimed by Brahmin beneficiaries, and land-grant administration. It also supplies a parallel text for correcting damaged portions of the Gauhati plates.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 247-260.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/16-Guwakuchi-Copper-Plates-Indrapala.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"40","inscription_name":"Gachtal Copper Plates Gopalavarman","date_display":"Approximately 1080 CE in Sharma’s chronology.","present_place":"Gachtal archaeological area, near Doboka, Hojai district, Assam, India","latitude":26.120015,"longitude":92.846318,"coordinate_precision":"Published district-government site reference","date_start_ce":1080.0,"date_end_ce":1080.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Gopalavarmadeva, son and successor of Indrapala.","findspot":"Gachtal near Dabaka, in the area described as Nowgong district; discovered in 1966.","event_or_recorded_subject":"Two surviving plates record a grant by Gopalavarmadeva of land capable of yielding 8,000 units of paddy. The donated land is named Khirikoukonci in Barasrojambubila visaya in the edition’s reading. The final plate and seal are missing, so the beneficiary’s name and concluding details are unavailable.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S17","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"within","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 261-278","summary_file":"17-Gachtal-Copper-Plates-Gopalavarman.txt","coordinate_type":"archaeological_site","coordinate_reference":"Gachtal / Doboka archaeological area","location_source":"https://hojai.assam.gov.in/tourist-place-detail/638","location_notes":"Government page supplies this reference for Gachtal at Doboka; the old copper-plate digging point is not separately established.","summary_text":"Gachtal Copper Plates Gopalavarman\n\nDate: Approximately 1080 CE in Sharma’s chronology.\nRuler: Gopalavarmadeva, son and successor of Indrapala.\nPlace: Gachtal near Dabaka, in the area described as Nowgong district; discovered in 1966.\n\nSUMMARY\nTwo surviving plates record a grant by Gopalavarmadeva of land capable of yielding 8,000 units of paddy. The donated land is named Khirikoukonci in Barasrojambubila visaya in the edition’s reading. The final plate and seal are missing, so the beneficiary’s name and concluding details are unavailable.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nImportant direct evidence for Gopala’s place in the Pala succession and for the administrative geography of his reign. It must be distinguished from other records associated with the name Gachtal.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 261-278.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/17-Gachtal-Copper-Plates-Gopalavarman.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"41","inscription_name":"Khanamukh Copper Plates Dharmapala","date_display":"First regnal year of Dharmapala; first half of the twelfth century in this edition’s historical framework.","present_place":"Khanamukh / Khonamukh, historical Barbhagia–Nagaon region, Assam, India; modern village match unresolved","latitude":26.35,"longitude":92.67,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — broad regional reference","date_start_ce":1100.0,"date_end_ce":1149.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Dharmapala, Kamarupa Pala dynasty; son of Harsapala and Ratna.","findspot":"Khanamukh in Barbhagia mauza, in the region described as Nowgong district.","event_or_recorded_subject":"A three-plate charter records a Brahmin grant assessed as yielding 6,000 units of paddy. It gives the royal genealogy, the beneficiary’s background and detailed boundaries using fields, trees, streams, neighbouring grants and settlements. Sharma discusses administrative terms and the geographical information preserved in it.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S18","regnal_or_era_date":"Regnal year 1","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"outside","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 279-294","summary_file":"18-Khanamukh-Copper-Plates-Dharmapala.txt","coordinate_type":"regional_reference","coordinate_reference":"Nagaon city, reference for historical Nagaon region","location_source":"https://www.pcbassam.org/SOURCE_STUDIES/NAGAON_NCAP_FINAL_REPORT_MARCH_2025.pdf","location_notes":"Broad regional reference only. The exact modern village has not been matched; proximity to Nagaon city is not established.","summary_text":"Khanamukh Copper Plates Dharmapala\n\nDate: First regnal year of Dharmapala; first half of the twelfth century in this edition’s historical framework.\nRuler: Dharmapala, Kamarupa Pala dynasty; son of Harsapala and Ratna.\nPlace: Khanamukh in Barbhagia mauza, in the region described as Nowgong district.\n\nSUMMARY\nA three-plate charter records a Brahmin grant assessed as yielding 6,000 units of paddy. It gives the royal genealogy, the beneficiary’s background and detailed boundaries using fields, trees, streams, neighbouring grants and settlements. Sharma discusses administrative terms and the geographical information preserved in it.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nA major source for the later Pala succession and rural administration. Its first-regnal-year date provides a relative chronological anchor, but converting it to an exact CE year depends on the chronology adopted.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 279-294.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/18-Khanamukh-Copper-Plates-Dharmapala.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"42","inscription_name":"Subhankarapataka Copper Plates Dharmapala","date_display":"Third regnal year of Dharmapala; first half of the twelfth century in this edition.","present_place":"Unknown findspot; Subhankarapataka is an ancient donated locality, not an established modern village","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"coordinate_precision":"Not georeferenced","date_start_ce":1100.0,"date_end_ce":1149.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Dharmapala, Kamarupa Pala dynasty.","findspot":"Exact findspot not established in the account; named after the donated locality.","event_or_recorded_subject":"The grant concerns Subhankarapataka in Dijjina visaya, including specified additional land. Its total yield is assessed at 6,000 units of paddy, divided between the brothers Himanga and Trilocana in shares of 4,000 and 2,000. Their family background and connections with Sravasti are described.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S19","regnal_or_era_date":"Regnal year 3","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"outside","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 295-308","summary_file":"19-Subhankarapataka-Copper-Plates-Dharmapala.txt","coordinate_type":"unresolved","coordinate_reference":"","location_source":"/home/angsuman/extra_spac/Primary source/Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","location_notes":"Ancient donated land and modern findspot are different concepts; both the modern identification and coordinates are unresolved.","summary_text":"Subhankarapataka Copper Plates Dharmapala\n\nDate: Third regnal year of Dharmapala; first half of the twelfth century in this edition.\nRuler: Dharmapala, Kamarupa Pala dynasty.\nPlace: Exact findspot not established in the account; named after the donated locality.\n\nSUMMARY\nThe grant concerns Subhankarapataka in Dijjina visaya, including specified additional land. Its total yield is assessed at 6,000 units of paddy, divided between the brothers Himanga and Trilocana in shares of 4,000 and 2,000. Their family background and connections with Sravasti are described.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nShows how a royal grant could be divided between related beneficiaries. It is useful for land assessment, Brahmin family history, administrative geography and the early regnal sequence of Dharmapala.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 295-308.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/19-Subhankarapataka-Copper-Plates-Dharmapala.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"43","inscription_name":"Puspabhadra Copper Plates Dharmapala","date_display":"Undated; assigned by Sharma to a later stage of Dharmapala’s reign, in the first half of the twelfth century.","present_place":"Puspabhadra riverbed, North Guwahati area, Assam, India; precise recovery point unresolved","latitude":26.197,"longitude":91.72,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — nearby reference, not exact findspot","date_start_ce":1100.0,"date_end_ce":1149.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Dharmapala, Kamarupa Pala dynasty.","findspot":"Dry bed of the Puspabhadra river at North Guwahati; reached Hem Chandra Goswami in 1909.","event_or_recorded_subject":"Dharmapala grants land assessed as yielding 10,000 units of paddy to the Brahmin Madhusudana, associated with the worship of Madhava. The charter opens with praise of Vishnu in his boar incarnation. Sharma credits the first eight verses to the king and the remainder to the poet Aniruddha.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S20","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"outside","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 309-327","summary_file":"20-Puspabhadra-Copper-Plates-Dharmapala.txt","coordinate_type":"nearby_town","coordinate_reference":"North Guwahati town","location_source":"https://www.geonames.org/1261186/north-guwahati.html","location_notes":"Town-level reference only, not the riverbed recovery point or Kanai Barasi Bowa rock.","summary_text":"Puspabhadra Copper Plates Dharmapala\n\nDate: Undated; assigned by Sharma to a later stage of Dharmapala’s reign, in the first half of the twelfth century.\nRuler: Dharmapala, Kamarupa Pala dynasty.\nPlace: Dry bed of the Puspabhadra river at North Guwahati; reached Hem Chandra Goswami in 1909.\n\nSUMMARY\nDharmapala grants land assessed as yielding 10,000 units of paddy to the Brahmin Madhusudana, associated with the worship of Madhava. The charter opens with praise of Vishnu in his boar incarnation. Sharma credits the first eight verses to the king and the remainder to the poet Aniruddha.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nNotable for substantial royal religious patronage, its explicitly Vaishnava opening and evidence of courtly literary composition. Sharma’s later-reign placement is an inference rather than a date stated in the inscription.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 309-327.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/20-Puspabhadra-Copper-Plates-Dharmapala.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"44","inscription_name":"Belava copper plates","date_display":"Twelfth century CE","present_place":"Belava, historical Rupganj–Narayanganj area, Bangladesh; exact village match unresolved","latitude":23.793,"longitude":90.517,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — broad regional reference","date_start_ce":1100.0,"date_end_ce":1199.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Bhojavarman; Jatavarman praised","findspot":"Belava, Bangladesh","event_or_recorded_subject":"A poetic passage about Jatavarman has been read as referring to the humiliation of Kamarupa.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Sharma questions that interpretation: a play on Kama and rupa may concern beauty rather than a political conquest.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"E09","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"External reference / identification","scope_350_1100":"outside","evidence_status":"Introduction notice image checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.16–0.17; supplied PDF p. 33","summary_file":"External inscriptions/E09-Belava-copper-plates.txt","coordinate_type":"regional_reference","coordinate_reference":"Rupganj upazila reference","location_source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rupganj_Upazila","location_notes":"Broad Rupganj reference for the historical Belava provenance, not a verified modern Belava village point.","summary_text":"Belava copper plates\n\nDate: Twelfth century CE\nRuler or persons named: Bhojavarman; Jatavarman praised\nPlace: Belava, Bangladesh\n\nSUMMARY\nA poetic passage about Jatavarman has been read as referring to the humiliation of Kamarupa.\n\nHOW IT RELATES TO KAMARUPA\nSharma questions that interpretation: a play on Kama and rupa may concern beauty rather than a political conquest.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.16–0.17, supplied PDF p. 33. This summary follows Sharma’s notice; the full external edition has not been re-edited here.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/External inscriptions/E09-Belava-copper-plates.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"45","inscription_name":"Deopara inscription","date_display":"Twelfth century CE","present_place":"Deopara, Godagari area, Rajshahi district, Bangladesh","latitude":24.44365,"longitude":88.5174,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate locality reference","date_start_ce":1100.0,"date_end_ce":1199.0,"material":"Stone","king_or_named_person":"Vijayasena","findspot":"Deopara, Rajshahi region, Bangladesh","event_or_recorded_subject":"Praises Vijayasena for driving away a Kamarupa king.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Sharma distinguishes repelling an opponent from subjugating the whole kingdom.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"E10","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"External reference / identification","scope_350_1100":"outside","evidence_status":"Introduction notice image checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.17; supplied PDF p. 34","summary_file":"External inscriptions/E10-Deopara-inscription.txt","coordinate_type":"village","coordinate_reference":"Deopara locality in Godagari, Rajshahi","location_source":"https://mapcarta.com/14754978","location_notes":"Locality match, not the stone slab current repository or a surveyed recovery point.","summary_text":"Deopara inscription\n\nDate: Twelfth century CE\nRuler or persons named: Vijayasena\nPlace: Deopara, Rajshahi region, Bangladesh\n\nSUMMARY\nPraises Vijayasena for driving away a Kamarupa king.\n\nHOW IT RELATES TO KAMARUPA\nSharma distinguishes repelling an opponent from subjugating the whole kingdom.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.17, supplied PDF p. 34. This summary follows Sharma’s notice; the full external edition has not been re-edited here.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/External inscriptions/E10-Deopara-inscription.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"46","inscription_name":"Madhainagar copper plates","date_display":"Twelfth century CE","present_place":"Madhainagar, historical Pabna region, Bangladesh; exact village match unresolved","latitude":24.0,"longitude":89.25,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — broad regional reference","date_start_ce":1100.0,"date_end_ce":1199.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Lakshmanasena","findspot":"Madhainagar, Bangladesh","event_or_recorded_subject":"Contains a claim concerning supremacy over a Kamarupa king.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Identifying the unnamed ruler with Rayarideva is a scholarly proposal. Outside the 350–1100 core.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"E11","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"External reference / identification","scope_350_1100":"outside","evidence_status":"Introduction notice image checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.17; supplied PDF p. 34","summary_file":"External inscriptions/E11-Madhainagar-copper-plates.txt","coordinate_type":"regional_reference","coordinate_reference":"Pabna town, historical regional reference","location_source":"https://www.getty.edu/vow/TGNFullDisplay?english=N&find=&nation=vietnam&place=&subjectid=1065870","location_notes":"Historical Pabna-region reference only. The modern Madhainagar location has not been established and may be distant from Pabna town.","summary_text":"Madhainagar copper plates\n\nDate: Twelfth century CE\nRuler or persons named: Lakshmanasena\nPlace: Madhainagar, Bangladesh\n\nSUMMARY\nContains a claim concerning supremacy over a Kamarupa king.\n\nHOW IT RELATES TO KAMARUPA\nIdentifying the unnamed ruler with Rayarideva is a scholarly proposal. Outside the 350–1100 core.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.17, supplied PDF p. 34. This summary follows Sharma’s notice; the full external edition has not been re-edited here.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/External inscriptions/E11-Madhainagar-copper-plates.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"47","inscription_name":"Kamauli Copper Plates Vaidyadeva","date_display":"Fourth regnal year of Vaidyadeva; approximately 1142 CE in Sharma’s edition.","present_place":"Kamauli near Varanasi, Uttar Pradesh, India; original village match unresolved","latitude":25.319,"longitude":83.013,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — nearby reference, not exact findspot","date_start_ce":1142.0,"date_end_ce":1142.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Vaidyadeva.","findspot":"Kamauli near Varanasi, Uttar Pradesh; discovered in 1891.","event_or_recorded_subject":"The charter grants the villages Santipataka and Mandara in the Kamarupa administrative hierarchy to the Brahmin Sridhara. It presents Vaidyadeva’s genealogy and achievements and describes his political connections with the Bengal Pala court. A Ganesha emblem appears on the seal.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S21","regnal_or_era_date":"Regnal year 4","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"outside","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 328-345","summary_file":"21-Kamauli-Copper-Plates-Vaidyadeva.txt","coordinate_type":"nearby_city","coordinate_reference":"Varanasi city","location_source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Varanasi","location_notes":"City reference for Kamauli near Varanasi; not one of the unverified villages with the same name.","summary_text":"Kamauli Copper Plates Vaidyadeva\n\nDate: Fourth regnal year of Vaidyadeva; approximately 1142 CE in Sharma’s edition.\nRuler: Vaidyadeva.\nPlace: Kamauli near Varanasi, Uttar Pradesh; discovered in 1891.\n\nSUMMARY\nThe charter grants the villages Santipataka and Mandara in the Kamarupa administrative hierarchy to the Brahmin Sridhara. It presents Vaidyadeva’s genealogy and achievements and describes his political connections with the Bengal Pala court. A Ganesha emblem appears on the seal.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nA key source for political developments following the Kamarupa Palas and for connections between Assam and Bengal. The findspot near Varanasi is distinct from the territory described in the grant.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 328-345.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/21-Kamauli-Copper-Plates-Vaidyadeva.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"48","inscription_name":"Rajghat inscription of Bhimadeva","date_display":"Mid-twelfth century context, approximately 1144–1161","present_place":"Rajghat, India; original edition needed to establish the modern findspot","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"coordinate_precision":"Not georeferenced","date_start_ce":1144.0,"date_end_ce":1161.0,"material":"Stone","king_or_named_person":"Bhimadeva, minister of Madanapala","findspot":"Rajghat; precise provenance to confirm in original edition","event_or_recorded_subject":"Praises Bhimadeva for saving Gauda or Varendra from an onslaught metaphorically described as waters or kings of Rayari’s lineage.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Poetic identification and political circumstances need the original edition. Outside the requested date range.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"E13","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"External reference / identification","scope_350_1100":"outside","evidence_status":"Introduction notice image checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.18; supplied PDF p. 35","summary_file":"External inscriptions/E13-Rajghat-inscription-of-Bhimadeva.txt","coordinate_type":"unresolved","coordinate_reference":"","location_source":"/home/angsuman/extra_spac/Primary source/Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","location_notes":"The broad locality is known, but a unique modern recovery point has not been verified. Coordinates intentionally blank.","summary_text":"Rajghat inscription of Bhimadeva\n\nDate: Mid-twelfth century context, approximately 1144–1161\nRuler or persons named: Bhimadeva, minister of Madanapala\nPlace: Rajghat; precise provenance to confirm in original edition\n\nSUMMARY\nPraises Bhimadeva for saving Gauda or Varendra from an onslaught metaphorically described as waters or kings of Rayari’s lineage.\n\nHOW IT RELATES TO KAMARUPA\nPoetic identification and political circumstances need the original edition. Outside the requested date range.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978, introduction p. 0.18, supplied PDF p. 35. This summary follows Sharma’s notice; the full external edition has not been re-edited here.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/External inscriptions/E13-Rajghat-inscription-of-Bhimadeva.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"49","inscription_name":"Assam Plates Vallabhadeva","date_display":"1185 CE.","present_place":"Tezpur area, Sonitpur district, Assam, India; reported collection provenance rather than a confirmed excavation point","latitude":26.63,"longitude":92.8,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate city reference","date_start_ce":1185.0,"date_end_ce":1185.0,"material":"Copper plates","king_or_named_person":"Vallabhadeva; genealogy names Bhaskara, Rayarideva and Udayakarna.","findspot":"Made known through Tezpur; also called the Tezpur plates of Vallabha.","event_or_recorded_subject":"A five-plate charter combines royal genealogy and praise with a charitable endowment and its conditions. It includes invocations to Ganesha and Vishnu. Sharma discusses the place names and the difficulty of fitting Vallabhadeva and his ancestors into the better-known sequence of Kamarupa rulers.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Date and interpretation follow Sharma (1978). CE dates reconstructed from regnal chronology are approximate, not dates written in CE.","source_url":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf","record_id":"S22","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Local / Kamarupa corpus","scope_350_1100":"outside","evidence_status":"Edition checked","source_locator":"M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam, Gauhati University, 1978; supplied PDF pp. 346-356","summary_file":"22-Assam-Plates-Vallabhadeva.txt","coordinate_type":"city","coordinate_reference":"Tezpur city","location_source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tezpur","location_notes":"Reported through Tezpur; a city coordinate does not prove the original findspot.","summary_text":"Assam Plates Vallabhadeva\n\nDate: 1185 CE.\nRuler: Vallabhadeva; genealogy names Bhaskara, Rayarideva and Udayakarna.\nPlace: Made known through Tezpur; also called the Tezpur plates of Vallabha.\n\nSUMMARY\nA five-plate charter combines royal genealogy and praise with a charitable endowment and its conditions. It includes invocations to Ganesha and Vishnu. Sharma discusses the place names and the difficulty of fitting Vallabhadeva and his ancestors into the better-known sequence of Kamarupa rulers.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nAn important late-twelfth-century record, but its precise political relationship to Vaidyadeva’s line is uncertain. Sharma notes that the text does not explicitly call Vallabha a king of Kamarupa or clearly identify his governmental seat.\n\nSource: M. M. Sharma, Inscriptions of Ancient Assam (1978), supplied PDF pages 346-356.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/22-Assam-Plates-Vallabhadeva.txt","source_href":"Inscriptions-of-Ancient-Assam-M-M-Sharma-1978.pdf"},{"list_order":"50","inscription_name":"Ambari inscription of Samudrapala","date_display":"Thirteenth century; 1232 CE proposed in Sanyal’s inventory","present_place":"Ambari archaeological site, Guwahati, Assam, India","latitude":26.185556,"longitude":91.755833,"coordinate_precision":"Published ASI excavation-site reference","date_start_ce":1200.0,"date_end_ce":1299.0,"material":"Rock / stone","king_or_named_person":"Samudrapala","findspot":"Ambari, Guwahati","event_or_recorded_subject":"Inscribed stone associated with Samudrapala.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"The exact date is not re-edited here. Earlier broad twelfth–thirteenth placement is not used to include it before 1100.","source_url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/361527651_Assam_from_the_Fifth_to_the_Thirteenth_Centuries","record_id":"L02","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Later regional context","scope_350_1100":"outside","evidence_status":"Catalogue / bibliography","source_locator":"Later-context inventory; original edition still needed","summary_file":"Later context/L02-Ambari-inscription-of-Samudrapala.txt","coordinate_type":"archaeological_site","coordinate_reference":"Ambari archaeological site","location_source":"https://asiguwahaticircle.gov.in/excavation8.html","location_notes":"Excavation site reference, not an inscription-specific locus or present museum custody.","summary_text":"Ambari inscription of Samudrapala\n\nDate: Thirteenth century; 1232 CE proposed in Sanyal’s inventory\nRuler or person named: Samudrapala\nPlace: Ambari, Guwahati\n\nSUMMARY\nInscribed stone associated with Samudrapala.\n\nLIMITS AND HISTORICAL SCOPE\nThe exact date is not re-edited here. Earlier broad twelfth–thirteenth placement is not used to include it before 1100.\nThis record is later than the 350–1100 CE focus. It is retained as context, not counted in that period.\n\nSource: https://www.researchgate.net/publication/361527651_Assam_from_the_Fifth_to_the_Thirteenth_Centuries\nThe original edition is still needed for a complete reading.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/Later context/L02-Ambari-inscription-of-Samudrapala.txt","source_href":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/361527651_Assam_from_the_Fifth_to_the_Thirteenth_Centuries"},{"list_order":"51","inscription_name":"Gachtal pillar inscription","date_display":"Later medieval; 1227 and later datings occur in the literature","present_place":"Gachtal archaeological site, near Doboka, Hojai district, Assam, India","latitude":26.120015,"longitude":92.846318,"coordinate_precision":"Published district-government site reference","date_start_ce":1200.0,"date_end_ce":1399.0,"material":"Rock / stone","king_or_named_person":"Not established here","findspot":"Gachtal, Nagaon","event_or_recorded_subject":"A pillar record distinct from Gopalavarman’s copper-plate charter.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"The date and reading need the original edition. It is excluded from the 350–1100 core regardless of the competing later dates.","source_url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/361527651_Assam_from_the_Fifth_to_the_Thirteenth_Centuries","record_id":"L03","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Later regional context","scope_350_1100":"outside","evidence_status":"Catalogue / bibliography","source_locator":"Later-context inventory; original edition still needed","summary_file":"Later context/L03-Gachtal-pillar-inscription.txt","coordinate_type":"archaeological_site","coordinate_reference":"Gachtal archaeological area","location_source":"https://hojai.assam.gov.in/tourist-place-detail/638","location_notes":"Find area, not present custody. The official page reports the stone inscription at District Museum Nagaon.","summary_text":"Gachtal pillar inscription\n\nDate: Later medieval; 1227 and later datings occur in the literature\nRuler or person named: Not established here\nPlace: Gachtal, Nagaon\n\nSUMMARY\nA pillar record distinct from Gopalavarman’s copper-plate charter.\n\nLIMITS AND HISTORICAL SCOPE\nThe date and reading need the original edition. It is excluded from the 350–1100 core regardless of the competing later dates.\nThis record is later than the 350–1100 CE focus. It is retained as context, not counted in that period.\n\nSource: https://www.researchgate.net/publication/361527651_Assam_from_the_Fifth_to_the_Thirteenth_Centuries\nThe original edition is still needed for a complete reading.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/Later context/L03-Gachtal-pillar-inscription.txt","source_href":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/361527651_Assam_from_the_Fifth_to_the_Thirteenth_Centuries"},{"list_order":"52","inscription_name":"Kanai Barasi Bowa rock inscription","date_display":"Saka 1127; conventionally 1206 CE","present_place":"Kanai Barasi Bowa / Rajaduar area, North Guwahati, Assam, India","latitude":26.197,"longitude":91.72,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — nearby reference, not exact findspot","date_start_ce":1206.0,"date_end_ce":1206.0,"material":"Rock / stone","king_or_named_person":"Local ruler not named","findspot":"North Guwahati","event_or_recorded_subject":"Records the destruction of Turks entering Kamarupa.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Does not itself name Prithu or Bakhtiyar Khalji. The Saka/CE conversion and the exact day should be checked in the original edition.","source_url":"https://gmda.assam.gov.in/sites/default/files/swf_utility_folder/departments/gmda_webcomindia_org_oid_4/do_u_want_2_know/forever_guwahati_1.pdf","record_id":"L01","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Later regional context","scope_350_1100":"outside","evidence_status":"Catalogue / bibliography","source_locator":"Later-context inventory; original edition still needed","summary_file":"Later context/L01-Kanai-Barasi-Bowa-rock-inscription.txt","coordinate_type":"nearby_town","coordinate_reference":"North Guwahati town","location_source":"https://www.geonames.org/1261186/north-guwahati.html","location_notes":"Town-level reference only, not the riverbed recovery point or Kanai Barasi Bowa rock.","summary_text":"Kanai Barasi Bowa rock inscription\n\nDate: Saka 1127; conventionally 1206 CE\nRuler or person named: Local ruler not named\nPlace: North Guwahati\n\nSUMMARY\nRecords the destruction of Turks entering Kamarupa.\n\nLIMITS AND HISTORICAL SCOPE\nDoes not itself name Prithu or Bakhtiyar Khalji. The Saka/CE conversion and the exact day should be checked in the original edition.\nThis record is later than the 350–1100 CE focus. It is retained as context, not counted in that period.\n\nSource: https://gmda.assam.gov.in/sites/default/files/swf_utility_folder/departments/gmda_webcomindia_org_oid_4/do_u_want_2_know/forever_guwahati_1.pdf\nThe original edition is still needed for a complete reading.\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/Later context/L01-Kanai-Barasi-Bowa-rock-inscription.txt","source_href":"https://gmda.assam.gov.in/sites/default/files/swf_utility_folder/departments/gmda_webcomindia_org_oid_4/do_u_want_2_know/forever_guwahati_1.pdf"},{"list_order":"53","inscription_name":"Kasomaripathar Krishna-Durga image inscription","date_display":"Early medieval attribution; precise date not verified","present_place":"Bishrampur, Kasomaripathar area, Golaghat district, Assam, India; exact modern point unresolved","latitude":26.52,"longitude":93.97,"coordinate_precision":"Approximate — broad regional reference","date_start_ce":null,"date_end_ce":null,"material":"Inscribed stone image","king_or_named_person":"Sri Jivara / Jivaraja identification proposed","findspot":"Kasomaripathar, Golaghat","event_or_recorded_subject":"Saikia’s discussion cites a Krishna-Durga image inscription in relation to the identification of Sri Jivaraja of the Palasini plate with Sri Jivara of the Deopani record.","dating_and_interpretation_notes":"Only a published reference has been checked. The original reading, date and catalogue identity remain to be verified. It is not counted in the dated core.","source_url":"https://abhidhajournal.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/Mrigakhee-Saikia.pdf","record_id":"B08","regnal_or_era_date":"","relation_to_kamarupa":"Regional / local inscription","scope_350_1100":"undated","evidence_status":"Published reference checked","source_locator":"Saikia 2021, printed p.39 / PDF p.16","summary_file":"Supplementary inscriptions/B08-Kasomaripathar-Krishna-Durga-image-inscription.txt","coordinate_type":"regional_reference","coordinate_reference":"Golaghat city, regional reference","location_source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golaghat","location_notes":"Broad Golaghat-region reference only. Bishrampur / Kasomaripathar has not been georeferenced and proximity to this point is not established.","summary_text":"Kasomaripathar Krishna-Durga image inscription\n\nDate: Early medieval attribution; precise date not verified\nRuler or named person: Sri Jivara / Jivaraja identification proposed\nPlace: Kasomaripathar, Golaghat\n\nSUMMARY\nSaikia’s discussion cites a Krishna-Durga image inscription in relation to the identification of Sri Jivaraja of the Palasini plate with Sri Jivara of the Deopani record.\n\nHISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nA further inscription to collect when investigating the Dhansiri valley and regional religious images.\n\nLIMITS OF THE EVIDENCE\nOnly a published reference has been checked. The original reading, date and catalogue identity remain to be verified. It is not counted in the dated core.\n\nSource: Saikia 2021, printed p.39 / PDF p.16\nSource location: https://abhidhajournal.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/Mrigakhee-Saikia.pdf\nEvidence checked: Published reference checked\n","summary_href":"Inscriptions of Ancient Assam - Separate Texts/Supplementary inscriptions/B08-Kasomaripathar-Krishna-Durga-image-inscription.txt","source_href":"https://abhidhajournal.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/Mrigakhee-Saikia.pdf"}],"source":"Kamarupa-Inscriptions-Chronology.csv","updated":"2026-11-01T13:57:46+00:00"};
